{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-815fabda0907640b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tI NTERPRETABLE MEG D ECODING OF P ERCEIVED S PEECH :\n2\tC ORTICAL S OURCES AND THE S TIMULUS F EATURES T HAT\n3\tD RIVE R ETRIEVAL\n4\t\n5\tIlia Semenkov\n6\tAXXX, Moscow, Russia\n7\tHSE University, Moscow, Russia\n8\tiliasemenkov@gmail.com\n9\t\n10\tDaria Kleeva\n11\tHSE University, Moscow, Russia\n12\t\n13\tarXiv:2608.01481v1 [cs.LG] 2 Aug 2026\n14\t\n15\tZarina Maksudova\n16\tITMO University, St. Petersburg, Russia\n17\t\n18\tIvan Dakhtin\n19\tHSE University, Moscow, Russia\n20\t\n21\tAlex Ossadtchi\n22\tHSE University, Moscow, Russia\n23\tAXXX, Moscow, Russia\n24\tossadtchi@gmail.com\n25\t\n26\tA BSTRACT\n27\tShort segments of perceived speech can be retrieved from non-invasive magnetoencephalographic\n28\t(MEG) recordings by a deep network trained with a CLIP-style objective against wav2vec 2.0 audio\n29\tembeddings [1–3]. Such studies do not convert their weights into the notions used in classical\n30\telectrophysiology. Although the spatial filters in the front end of [1] could in principle be mapped to\n31\tsource topographies, the dynamic properties of those sources remain out of reach. Which properties\n32\tof the speech stream contribute to the decoding is equally unclear.\n33\tHere we scrutinize a decoder whose front end is constrained by the physics of the measurement and\n34\tby the physiology of its sources. Building on the framework of Petrosyan et al. [4, 5], we replace the\n35\t2D Fourier spatial attention of [1] with a layer parameterized by spherical harmonics [6], reduce the\n36\tsubject-specific representation from 270 to K = 25 branches, add a layer of trainable temporal filters,\n37\tso that each branch in our network is matched to a neuronal source in time as well as in space. Ocular\n38\tand cardiac components are removed before training, since either could supply stimulus-locked\n39\tinformation that would otherwise be mistaken for cortical.\n40\tOn the cleaned MEG-MASC dataset [7] the model reaches 39.75 ± 0.34% Top-1 accuracy among\n41\t1005 candidates across six trained solutions, with approximately 20× fewer trainable parameters\n42\tin the decoder. Its weights map to source space [4, 8], recovering generators consistent with the\n43\tcanonical speech-perception network, and branches localizing to the left carry higher frequency\n44\trhythmic components not evident on the right. Paired MEG occlusion, replacing feature-marked\n45\tspeech segments with matched donors from feature-present and feature-absent intervals, shows that\n46\t15 of 19 stimulus features contribute, the largest effects being silence, sound intensity, vowels and\n47\tacoustic onsets. Intriguingly, the randomly ordered word lists behave oppositely: narrative MEG\n48\tsubstituted into them improves retrieval, so activity elicited by words stripped of narrative structure\n49\tcarries less recoverable information than the activity elicited by coherent speech. The wav2vec target\n50\tcan be reduced to about twelve learned feature dimensions without loss of retrieval accuracy, whereas\n51\tstrong temporal compression causes a clear performance loss.\n52\tPhysically and physiologically constrained decoders can thus serve as knowledge-discovery tools:\n53\ttheir learned weights can be mapped to cortical sources and temporal dynamics, while input interventions reveal what the decision rule relies on.\n54\tProject page: https://ivsemenkov.github.io/LISA/\n55\t\n56\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n57\t\n58\tKeywords Magnetoencephalography · Speech perception · Neural decoding · Interpretable deep learning ·\n59\tSpatial–temporal decomposition · Source localization · Occlusion analysis · Naturalistic stimuli\n60\t\n61\t1\n62\t\n63\tIntroduction\n64\t\n65\tDeep networks can now retrieve short segments of perceived speech from magnetoencephalography (MEG). Given\n66\tthree seconds of brain activity, they pick the matching audio out of more than a thousand candidates [1]. This naturally\n67\tinvites the conclusion that rich linguistic content can be recovered from the healthy brain without surgery.\n68\tAccuracy alone, though, does not tell us what the network detected. These models turn sensor signals into speech\n69\tembeddings through a chain of transformations, and the resulting weights match nothing an electrophysiologist can\n70\tname: not a cortical location, not a rhythm, not a time course. The measurement and its interpretation therefore describe\n71\tdifferent things, and no gain in accuracy will connect them. The concern is not ours alone. Writing about their own\n72\tearlier work, d’Ascoli et al. [9] say that “it is unclear whether the decoder relies on the perceptual characteristics of the\n73\tspeech segment or the semantic features of the underlying words”. Recent work has analyzed the performance in this\n74\tretrieval setting [10], but it did not identify which properties of the speech stream the decoder uses. That question can\n75\tbe settled by intervening directly on the decoder’s MEG input, which requires no particular architecture. Where in\n76\tcortex that information arises, and with what dynamics, is a different question, and answering it does require a decoder\n77\twhose weights can be read.\n78\tThis matters because we increasingly ask decoding models to do science rather than only to score well. Classical studies\n79\tof speech perception compare a handful of carefully chosen conditions and fit simple statistical models to the differences\n80\tbetween them. The control is excellent, but the design suits continuous natural speech poorly. Machine learning offers\n81\tanother route: set a hard decoding task on natural recordings, then examine the rule the network learned. This reverses\n82\tthe usual order of an experiment. Instead of deciding in advance which stimulus features ought to matter and then\n83\ttesting them, we let the network use whatever helps and afterward ask what it used, and which neural populations\n84\tsupplied it.\n85\tThe phenomenon of speech perception suits this approach well. MEG provides millisecond temporal resolution and\n86\twhole-head coverage [11, 12]. Fifty years of invasive and non-invasive work have described the cortical networks\n87\tinvolved [13–15]. And because speech production and perception share circuitry [16–19], the applications are concrete —\n88\tspeech neuroprostheses [5, 20], imagined-speech interfaces [21], and passive language mapping during surgery [22–24].\n89\tExplainable AI has begun to reach neuroimaging. Recent work has audited EEG foundation models with sparse\n90\tautoencoders [25], tracked how functionally specialized units emerge in networks trained on brain signals [26], and\n91\tused causal tracing to find the pathways inside brain-to-speech models [27]. These methods say useful things about\n92\twhat a network represents internally. What they do not do is connect those representations to the physics that produced\n93\tthe data — the forward model that carries cortical currents to the sensors. The studies that tackle speech or complex\n94\tmovement also work on intracranial recordings [26, 27], while the non-invasive work targets broad clinical categories\n95\trather than language [25]. Nobody has yet combined strong decoding of complex natural behavior from non-invasive\n96\tdata with an interpretation that lands on specific sources and their dynamics. Without that link, the move from an\n97\taccuracy figure to a neuroscientific claim stays risky: we get high numbers and little idea what they mean.\n98\tA second line of work avoids the problem by building interpretability into the architecture. Compact models such as\n99\tEEGNet [28], ShallowNet [29] and LF-CNN [30] separate spatial from temporal filtering, which mirrors how the data\n100\tarise: band-limited activity in a neural population reaches the sensors through the forward operator. Those models were\n101\tbuilt for simpler classification and regression problems, and earlier attempts to interpret them missed one point — the\n102\tspatial and temporal filters adapt at the same time, and each changes what the other sees. Petrosyan et al. [4] corrected\n103\tthis and derived how to map jointly trained weights onto source locations and their dynamics. The same front end later\n104\tdrove a decoder for invasive ECoG and sEEG speech decoding [5]. Its advantage over post-hoc explanation is that the\n105\tinterpretation refers to the forward model of the MEG signal, not to latent features and a heuristic for ranking them. We\n106\textend it here to whole-head MEG from many subjects during a demanding language task.\n107\tWe start from Défossez et al. [1], who paired a spatial-attention layer with subject-specific spatial layers so that spatial\n108\tfilters could be trained across participants, then passed the result through convolutional layers to approximate wav2vec\n109\t2.0 audio embeddings [2] under a contrastive objective [3]. We change four things. First, their spatial attention uses 2D\n110\tFourier functions over a flattened projection of the sensor array; we use real spherical harmonics instead [6]. MEG\n111\tsensors sit on a roughly spherical helmet, so this is the natural basis for the field they measure. Our ablations show that\n112\tsome attention layer is needed — removing it altogether costs four to five percentage points — and that the spherical\n113\tparameterization is worth about one further point over the planar one, which is larger than the seed-to-seed variability\n114\treported below. The main argument for it, though, is physical rather than empirical. Second, we add trainable depthwise\n115\t2\n116\t\n117\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n118\t\n119\ttemporal filters after the subject block, so that each branch matches a source in time as well as in space. Third, we cut\n120\tthe latent representation to K = 25 branches, which imposes a small and physiologically plausible bottleneck. Finally,\n121\tone change concerns the data rather than the model: we remove ocular and cardiac components before training. These\n122\tartifacts are a plausible route to shortcut learning here, because eye activity tracks linguistic structure and attended\n123\tspeech even without corresponding visual input [31–33], while cardiac dynamics vary with narrative intensity and\n124\tconscious narrative processing [34, 35]. Left in the data, they could supply the same stimulus-locked acoustic or\n125\tlinguistic information that our occlusion analysis later probes.\n126\tOn MEG-MASC [7] the model retrieves the correct three-second segment among 1005 candidates with 39.8% Top-1\n127\tand 70.4% Top-10 accuracy, averaged over six independently trained solutions. The complete MEG decoder contains\n128\t486,619 trainable parameters, approximately 20× fewer than the brain decoder of Défossez et al. [1] (Appendix C).\n129\tThese figures cannot be compared directly with those reported by Défossez et al. [1], whose test segments are aligned\n130\tto word onsets and whose reported preprocessing does not explicitly remove ocular or cardiac components. Within our\n131\town ablation grid, where the data and test set are held fixed, the K = 270, five-block configuration—the closest tested\n132\tmodel in branch count and decoder depth—is 14.8 times larger than the main model and scores 3.60 percentage points\n133\tlower in Top-1 accuracy (Appendix C).\n134\tWe first ask where in cortex the information supporting retrieval arises, by mapping the trained spatial and temporal\n135\tweights to source space. We then ask which properties of the speech stream that information corresponds to, by\n136\tintervening on the decoder’s input with paired MEG substitutions in which feature-marked intervals are replaced by\n137\tmatched real-MEG donors. The Methods develop the interpretation framework and the substitution procedure. The\n138\tResults report the recovered cortical sources, the stimulus features the decoder relies on, and a set of compression and\n139\tablation experiments that bound both.\n140\tWith this we hope to demonstrate that an architecture equipped with explicit physical and physiological priors loses no\n141\taccuracy and can still be inspected once trained. Its weights convert into cortical sources and their dynamics, while\n142\tpaired input interventions reveal which stimulus-linked information the trained decoder uses. Decoders built this way\n143\tcan serve as instruments for studying the brain, not only as benchmarks for decoding it.\n144\t\n145\t2\n146\t\n147\tTheoretical background\n148\t\n149\tThis section establishes the theoretical and methodological framework for modeling MEG data and identifying taskrelated neural sources. We first introduce the biophysical generative model of MEG signals and review classical spatial\n150\tfiltering techniques used to estimate source activity from sensor measurements. Following a discussion on the trade-offs\n151\tbetween traditional approaches and modern deep learning methods, we briefly outline the mathematically grounded\n152\tframework of Petrosyan et al. [4] for interpreting deep neural networks with factorized spatial-temporal layers. By\n153\tlinking learned network weights back to the spatial topographies and temporal dynamics of the underlying neural\n154\tpopulations, this methodology enables the extraction of physiologically meaningful features during complex decoding\n155\ttasks, which we subsequently apply to the decoding of perceived speech.\n156\t2.1\n157\t\n158\tMEG generative equation\n159\t\n160\tThe main cortical contributors to MEG signals are intracellular currents associated with synaptic activity in pyramidal\n161\tneurons. When many similarly oriented pyramidal neurons within a cortical patch are active synchronously, their\n162\tcombined contribution can be approximated by a single equivalent current dipole (ECD) [12, 36] with location vector\n163\trn = [xn , yn , zn ]⊤ and orientation θ n = [θnx , θny , θnz ]⊤ , where n indexes the neuronal source. The time-varying\n164\tamplitude of the n-th ECD is denoted by sn (t).\n165\tThe array of M MEG sensors surrounding the head and located at positions rm , m = 1, . . . , M , samples at each time\n166\tinstant t a vector x(t) = [x1 (t), . . . , xM (t)]⊤ of the weak magnetic field produced by the superposition of the magnetic\n167\tfields generated by the activation moments s(t) = [s1 (t), . . . , sN (t)]⊤ , where N is the number of task-related neuronal\n168\tsources.\n169\tThe signal generated by a single n-th source can be modeled as x(t) = gn sn (t), where gn = g(rn , θ n ) is the\n170\tM × 1 gain vector mapping the activity of the unit dipole at rn with orientation θ n to the sensors. Visualizing gn\n171\tby interpolating its values between sensor locations gives the so-called topography of the source. The collection of\n172\tgain vectors for a grid of candidate cortical locations and orientations is obtained by solving Maxwell’s equations for\n173\tthe head as a volume conductor on the basis of the geometric information about MEG sensors and cortical sources\n174\textracted from MRI using standard tools [37].\n175\t3\n176\t\n177\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n178\t\n179\tWhen multiple sources are active, linearity of Maxwell’s equations gives\n180\tx(t) =\n181\t\n182\tN\n183\tX\n184\t\n185\tgn sn (t) +\n186\t\n187\tn=1\n188\t\n189\tNu\n190\tX\n191\t\n192\tqu pu (t) + n(t),\n193\t\n194\t(1)\n195\t\n196\tu=1\n197\t\n198\tThe first summation in (1) corresponds to the task-related sources whose activity via some potentially complex non-linear\n199\tmapping predicts our behavioral variable z(t). The second sum describes the contribution of the Nu task-unrelated\n200\tsources to the measured data x(t). The latter can be regarded as the spatially-correlated brain noise term. The last\n201\telement in this equation n(t) is the spatially-white sensor noise.\n202\tA typical brain decoding task involves designing an algorithm that operates on the sensor measurements x(t) to\n203\tinfer our target behavioral variable z(t). We assume z(t) depends (possibly non-linearly) only on the task-related\n204\tactivity, i.e. on a subset of source activations si (t), i = 1, . . . , N whose contributions are mediated through the\n205\tcorresponding topographies gi . All information about z(t) available from x(t) is therefore confined to the subspace\n206\tS = span{g1 , . . . , gN } spanned by the topographies gn of these task-related sources. Crucially, since S is in general\n207\tnot orthogonal to the brain-noise subspace I = span{q1 , . . . , qNu }, an effective decoder must not only tune to S but\n208\talso tune away from I.\n209\t2.2\n210\t\n211\tFrom sensor signals to source activity\n212\t\n213\tThe standard tool used in classical MEG analysis to access the activity of neuronal sources sn (t) is a spatial filter wk⊤\n214\tapplied to the channel time series:\n215\tŝk (t) = wk⊤ x(t) =\n216\t\n217\tN\n218\tX\n219\t\n220\twk⊤ gn sn (t) +\n221\t\n222\tn=1\n223\t\n224\tNu\n225\tX\n226\t\n227\twk⊤ qu pu (t) + wk⊤ n(t).\n228\t\n229\t(2)\n230\t\n231\tu=1\n232\t\n233\tClearly, the vector wk designed to estimate the activity of the k-th source must, on the one hand, be aligned with the\n234\tcorresponding source topography gk , i.e. wk⊤ gn > 0 for k = n. On the other hand, it must be tuned away from the\n235\ttopographies of all other sources, both task-related and task-unrelated, so that their contributions do not leak into the\n236\testimate of the k-th source activity, i.e. wk⊤ gn ≈ 0 for k ̸= n and wk⊤ qu ≈ 0 for u = 1, . . . , Nu . Given the limited\n237\tnumber of degrees of freedom and the inherently limited rank of multichannel EEG or MEG measurements, we are\n238\ttypically faced with a trade-off between rejecting interference and tuning to the target source.\n239\tUsing the orthogonality principle of estimation theory [38], it can be shown that, when the additive noise and taskunrelated activity are uncorrelated with the task-related signals, the optimal filter matrix W = [w1 , w2 , . . . , wN ] tuned\n240\tto the sources with topographies G = [g1 , g2 , . . . , gN ] and accounting for the spatial structure of the interference in\n241\tx(t) can be expressed as\n242\tW = R−1\n243\tx GRs ,\n244\t\n245\t(3)\n246\t\n247\twhere Rx = E x(t)x(t)⊤ is the M × M data covariance matrix and Rs is the N × N covariance matrix of the\n248\tsource signals sn (t), n = 1, . . . , N .\n249\t\b\n250\t\n251\tIn practice, we don’t know the pivotal sources and their topographies and it is the goal of a typical neuroscientific study\n252\tto find them. We are often interested in sources whose activity predicts a specific behavioral variable, such as limb\n253\tkinematics or a perceived or produced audio signal, or reflects differences between experimental conditions. In doing\n254\tso we can opt for three main strategies: exhaustive search, optimal filtering, or deep learning. The exhaustive search\n255\tapproach relies on the rows of an EEG or MEG inverse operator to define spatial filters W. The primary advantage of\n256\tthis method is its high interpretability, as the resulting filters correspond directly to specific neural source locations.\n257\tHowever, this approach requires a highly accurate inverse operator, suffers from low statistical sensitivity due to the\n258\tmultiple comparisons problem, and is generally not designed to handle complex decoding tasks but rather to perform\n259\tbinary contrasts of several experimental conditions.\n260\tTo circumvent the computational burden of the exhaustive search strategy, optimal spatial filtering provides a task-driven\n261\talternative. In this framework, spatial filters are learned directly from the data according to method-specific objectives\n262\t[39–41], making the process highly efficient — source localization via an inverse operator is only required as an\n263\toptional final step. Nevertheless, this approach is still not well suited to complex decoding tasks. By constraining the\n264\tbehavioral variable to be a simple function of the filter output or its envelope, it lacks the expressivity required to model\n265\tintricate, non-linear mappings between neural activity and behavior.\n266\tFinally, deep learning represents a fully adaptive, data-driven paradigm where the spatial filters are learned from the\n267\tdata as a part of a more general decoder tuned to solve a classification or regression task. This approach is suited to\n268\t4\n269\t\n270\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n271\t\n272\tFigure 1: Interpretable front-end made as a collection of branches, the k-th branch is highlighted in blue. During\n273\ttraining each branch gets matched to a neural source with specific spatial and dynamical properties.\n274\tcomplex tasks as it connects source activity estimates to the behavioral variable via a complex learnable transformation.\n275\tHowever, these powerful capabilities come with significant trade-offs: deep learning models require massive amounts\n276\tof training data and act somewhat like a \"black box,\" making it notoriously difficult to link the learned features back to\n277\tspecific anatomical sources and their underlying dynamic properties.\n278\tThis shortcoming can be overcome by using an interpretable, physiologically grounded front end that allows us to relate\n279\tthe parameters of the trained network to the neural sources whose activity is pivotal for solving the decoding task.\n280\t2.3\n281\t\n282\tInterpretable neural architectures with factorized spatial-temporal layers\n283\t\n284\tSeveral compact deep neural network architectures have been developed to decode EEG and MEG activity by employing\n285\tfactorized spatial and temporal filtering operations. Notable examples include EEGNet [28], DeepConvNet and its\n286\tcompact variant ShallowConvNet [29], the separable CNN proposed by Waytowich et al. [42], as well as LF-CNN and\n287\tVAR-CNN [30].\n288\tIt is noteworthy that all these architectures contain not only the trainable spatial filters considered above but also the\n289\ttemporal filters that can be tuned to specific dynamical properties of the activity of neuronal sources. While these\n290\tmodels successfully leverage separated spatial and temporal processing stages to perform automatic feature extraction\n291\twith a minimal number of parameters, none of these earlier studies provided a rigorous interpretation of the learned\n292\tweights. Specifically, the previous interpretation attempts overlooked the fact that in such factorized architectures, both\n293\tthe spatial and temporal filters adapt concurrently during the training procedure.\n294\tTo address this limitation, Petrosyan et al. [4] introduced a theoretically justified framework that provides the proper\n295\trecipe for interpreting these weights, demonstrating that accurate reconstruction of the underlying neuronal sources\n296\tand their dynamic properties requires accounting for the mutual dependence of the spatial and temporal filters. The\n297\tdescribed modification is apparent if one views the front-end that comprises factorized spatial and temporal filters as a\n298\tcollection of branches where each one adapts to (gets matched to) a particular neural source with specific spatial and\n299\tdynamical properties, see Figure 1. The k-th branch performs the following operation\n300\t\u0001\n301\trk (t) = f wk⊤ x(t) ∗ hk (t) ,\n302\t\n303\t(4)\n304\t\n305\twhere ∗ denotes 1D temporal convolution, hk (t) is a T -tap impulse response of the k-th branch temporal filter and f (·)\n306\tis an optional element-wise non-linearity.\n307\tApplying equation (3) to the branch operation equation (4), taking into account the fact that the spatial and temporal\n308\tprocessing occur within a mutual context and stating that Rs is unknown in practice, we can arrive at the following\n309\trecipe for deriving the corresponding spatial and temporal patterns from the learned filter weights.\n310\tThe estimate of the spatial pattern of the k-th pivotal source is computed as\n311\tĝk ∝ Rhk wk ,\n312\t⊤\n313\t\n314\t(5)\n315\t\n316\twhere Rhk = E{(x(t).∗ hk (τ ))(x(t).∗ hk (τ )) } is the covariance of the multichannel data filtered with the temporal\n317\tfilter of the k-th branch and .∗ denotes per-channel convolution. The intuition here is that the spatial filter, while\n318\t5\n319\t\n320\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n321\t\n322\ttuning away from the interfering sources, takes into account the extent to which the contribution of these interfering\n323\tsources is eliminated by the temporal filter operating within the same branch and therefore uses the accordingly filtered\n324\tmultichannel data to compute the spatial covariance matrix.\n325\tTo map the branch-specific topographies ĝk onto the cortex, one can choose from a variety of inverse-modeling\n326\tapproaches [43]. In this work, we used the Minimum Norm Estimate (MNE) [44]. In its basic form, the corresponding\n327\tlinear inverse operator can be written as\n328\t⊤\n329\t2\n330\tWMNE = G⊤\n331\tall Gall Gall + λ I\n332\t\n333\t\u0001−1\n334\t\n335\t,\n336\t\n337\t(6)\n338\t\n339\twhere Gall ∈ RM ×P contains the sensor topographies of all source components included in the forward model, and P\n340\tdepends on the cortical source space and orientation model. The specific noise covariance, depth weighting, orientation\n341\tconstraint, and regularization used for the reported cortical maps are described in Section 3.4.\n342\tAnalysis of each branch also yields a temporal pattern describing the second-order temporal structure selected by that\n343\tbranch. Let ak (t) = wk⊤ x(t) + bk be the spatially filtered branch signal and let ak (t) = [ak (t), ak (t − 1), . . . , ak (t −\n344\tT + 1)]⊤ denote its lag-embedded representation. Using an ordering of the temporal-filter coefficients consistent with\n345\tthis lag vector, the temporal pattern is computed as\n346\tp̂k ∝ Rak hk ,\n347\t\n348\tRak = Cov[ak (t)] .\n349\t\n350\t(7)\n351\t\n352\tIn the reported analyses, Rak was estimated with Ledoit–Wolf shrinkage and T = 15. The magnitude spectrum\n353\t|F{p̂k }| was used as a descriptive frequency-domain representation of this temporal pattern. Thus, each branch is\n354\tcharacterized by a spatial pattern ĝk and a temporal pattern p̂k derived while accounting for the covariance structure of\n355\tthe corresponding filtered signals.\n356\tAlthough the number K of effective branches representing the neuronal sources necessary for decoding is typically\n357\tunknown a priori, it can be inferred iteratively. Specifically, K is identified as the minimum number of branches\n358\trequired before decoding performance plateaus or begins to degrade. Following Petrosyan et al. [4], we can treat K\n359\tas the estimate of N in equation (1). Once K is established, we can estimate the task-informative signal subspace\n360\tas the K-dimensional subspace Ŝ = span{ĝ1 , . . . , ĝK } within the M -dimensional measurement space. This is the\n361\tsubspace the trained network restricts its attention to in order to solve the posed decoding task. Similarly to the\n362\tindividual topographies forming this subspace, the signal subspace as a whole can be scrutinized in the source space\n363\tusing subspace matching approaches such as RAP-MUSIC [45] or its later versions.\n364\tHistorically, the majority of interpretable, compact deep neural network architectures utilizing factorized spatialtemporal filtering—such as those proposed by Lawhern et al. [28], DeepConvNet [29], separable CNNs [42], and\n365\tLF-CNN/VAR-CNN [30]—have been applied to relatively simple classification or regression tasks. These models\n366\ttypically lacked sophisticated decoder heads beyond their factorized blocks; at most, architectures like DeepConvNet\n367\temployed a simple succession of convolutional layers. However, as demonstrated by Petrosyan et al. [5], such\n368\tinterpretable front-ends can be successfully adapted for highly complex tasks like overt speech decoding. In their\n369\tapproach, a recurrent neural network processed the outputs from the spatial-temporal filtering block to predict speech\n370\tmel-spectrograms from ongoing brain activity immediately preceding an utterance. Subsequent localization of the pivotal\n371\tneural sources—derived from the individual branches of the network’s front-end—successfully identified languagerelated cortical sites whose locations showed correspondence with regions where direct electrical stimulation induced\n372\tspeech arrest and production errors, supporting the physiological relevance of the extracted features. Furthermore, the\n373\tanalysis of temporal patterns revealed a relationship between compact spatial patterns and high central frequencies.\n374\tThis relationship is highly characteristic of true neural activity, confirming that the model’s predictions were driven by\n375\tgenuine brain signals rather than being confounded by muscular artifacts.\n376\tIn the current work we apply the methodology of Petrosyan et al. [4] to the task of decoding perceived speech from the\n377\tconcurrently recorded MEG data.\n378\t\n379\t3\n380\t\n381\tMethods\n382\t\n383\tAs stated earlier, in this work we solve the retrieval task in which the network learns to construct embeddings for MEG\n384\tdata similar to the audio embeddings produced by wav2vec 2.0 [2], with a CLIP-style objective [3], following [1].\n385\tOur architecture, see Figure 2 inherits the overall block structure of [1] but replaces its spatial-attention layer with a\n386\tphysically motivated one, augments it with a temporal-filtering layer in the spirit of [4, 5] and modifies a convolutional\n387\tdecoder.\n388\t6\n389\t\n390\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n391\t\n392\tInterpretable Front-End\n393\tMEG data\n394\tM = 208\n395\tchannels,\n396\t3 seconds\n397\t\n398\tConvolutional Module\n399\tTemporal\n400\tFiltering (Linear)\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b9c66524-a39d-41df-a83a-b672ae01d3f5","uuid":"72e42db3-26d5-44c5-8b11-3384bd7c56a2","timestamp":"2026-08-04T15:48:05.583Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/2608.01481v1.txt","content":"I NTERPRETABLE MEG D ECODING OF P ERCEIVED S PEECH :\nC ORTICAL S OURCES AND THE S TIMULUS F EATURES T HAT\nD RIVE R ETRIEVAL\n\nIlia Semenkov\nAXXX, Moscow, Russia\nHSE University, Moscow, Russia\niliasemenkov@gmail.com\n\nDaria Kleeva\nHSE University, Moscow, Russia\n\narXiv:2608.01481v1 [cs.LG] 2 Aug 2026\n\nZarina Maksudova\nITMO University, St. Petersburg, Russia\n\nIvan Dakhtin\nHSE University, Moscow, Russia\n\nAlex Ossadtchi\nHSE University, Moscow, Russia\nAXXX, Moscow, Russia\nossadtchi@gmail.com\n\nA BSTRACT\nShort segments of perceived speech can be retrieved from non-invasive magnetoencephalographic\n(MEG) recordings by a deep network trained with a CLIP-style objective against wav2vec 2.0 audio\nembeddings [1–3]. Such studies do not convert their weights into the notions used in classical\nelectrophysiology. Although the spatial filters in the front end of [1] could in principle be mapped to\nsource topographies, the dynamic properties of those sources remain out of reach. Which properties\nof the speech stream contribute to the decoding is equally unclear.\nHere we scrutinize a decoder whose front end is constrained by the physics of the measurement and\nby the physiology of its sources. Building on the framework of Petrosyan et al. [4, 5], we replace the\n2D Fourier spatial attention of [1] with a layer parameterized by spherical harmonics [6], reduce the\nsubject-specific representation from 270 to K = 25 branches, add a layer of trainable temporal filters,\nso that each branch in our network is matched to a neuronal source in time as well as in space. Ocular\nand cardiac components are removed before training, since either could supply stimulus-locked\ninformation that would otherwise be mistaken for cortical.\nOn the cleaned MEG-MASC dataset [7] the model reaches 39.75 ± 0.34% Top-1 accuracy among\n1005 candidates across six trained solutions, with approximately 20× fewer trainable parameters\nin the decoder. Its weights map to source space [4, 8], recovering generators consistent with the\ncanonical speech-perception network, and branches localizing to the left carry higher frequency\nrhythmic components not evident on the right. Paired MEG occlusion, replacing feature-marked\nspeech segments with matched donors from feature-present and feature-absent intervals, shows that\n15 of 19 stimulus features contribute, the largest effects being silence, sound intensity, vowels and\nacoustic onsets. Intriguingly, the randomly ordered word lists behave oppositely: narrative MEG\nsubstituted into them improves retrieval, so activity elicited by words stripped of narrative structure\ncarries less recoverable information than the activity elicited by coherent speech. The wav2vec target\ncan be reduced to about twelve learned feature dimensions without loss of retrieval accuracy, whereas\nstrong temporal compression causes a clear performance loss.\nPhysically and physiologically constrained decoders can thus serve as knowledge-discovery tools:\ntheir learned weights can be mapped to cortical sources and temporal dynamics, while input interventions reveal what the decision rule relies on.\nProject page: https://ivsemenkov.github.io/LISA/\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\nKeywords Magnetoencephalography · Speech perception · Neural decoding · Interpretable deep learning ·\nSpatial–temporal decomposition · Source localization · Occlusion analysis · Naturalistic stimuli\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nDeep networks can now retrieve short segments of perceived speech from magnetoencephalography (MEG). Given\nthree seconds of brain activity, they pick the matching audio out of more than a thousand candidates [1]. This naturally\ninvites the conclusion that rich linguistic content can be recovered from the healthy brain without surgery.\nAccuracy alone, though, does not tell us what the network detected. These models turn sensor signals into speech\nembeddings through a chain of transformations, and the resulting weights match nothing an electrophysiologist can\nname: not a cortical location, not a rhythm, not a time course. The measurement and its interpretation therefore describe\ndifferent things, and no gain in accuracy will connect them. The concern is not ours alone. Writing about their own\nearlier work, d’Ascoli et al. [9] say that “it is unclear whether the decoder relies on the perceptual characteristics of the\nspeech segment or the semantic features of the underlying words”. Recent work has analyzed the performance in this\nretrieval setting [10], but it did not identify which properties of the speech stream the decoder uses. That question can\nbe settled by intervening directly on the decoder’s MEG input, which requires no particular architecture. Where in\ncortex that information arises, and with what dynamics, is a different question, and answering it does require a decoder\nwhose weights can be read.\nThis matters because we increasingly ask decoding models to do science rather than only to score well. Classical studies\nof speech perception compare a handful of carefully chosen conditions and fit simple statistical models to the differences\nbetween them. The control is excellent, but the design suits continuous natural speech poorly. Machine learning offers\nanother route: set a hard decoding task on natural recordings, then examine the rule the network learned. This reverses\nthe usual order of an experiment. Instead of deciding in advance which stimulus features ought to matter and then\ntesting them, we let the network use whatever helps and afterward ask what it used, and which neural populations\nsupplied it.\nThe phenomenon of speech perception suits this approach well. MEG provides millisecond temporal resolution and\nwhole-head coverage [11, 12]. Fifty years of invasive and non-invasive work have described the cortical networks\ninvolved [13–15]. And because speech production and perception share circuitry [16–19], the applications are concrete —\nspeech neuroprostheses [5, 20], imagined-speech interfaces [21], and passive language mapping during surgery [22–24].\nExplainable AI has begun to reach neuroimaging. Recent work has audited EEG foundation models with sparse\nautoencoders [25], tracked how functionally specialized units emerge in networks trained on brain signals [26], and\nused causal tracing to find the pathways inside brain-to-speech models [27]. These methods say useful things about\nwhat a network represents internally. What they do not do is connect those representations to the physics that produced\nthe data — the forward model that carries cortical currents to the sensors. The studies that tackle speech or complex\nmovement also work on intracranial recordings [26, 27], while the non-invasive work targets broad clinical categories\nrather than language [25]. Nobody has yet combined strong decoding of complex natural behavior from non-invasive\ndata with an interpretation that lands on specific sources and their dynamics. Without that link, the move from an\naccuracy figure to a neuroscientific claim stays risky: we get high numbers and little idea what they mean.\nA second line of work avoids the problem by building interpretability into the architecture. Compact models such as\nEEGNet [28], ShallowNet [29] and LF-CNN [30] separate spatial from temporal filtering, which mirrors how the data\narise: band-limited activity in a neural population reaches the sensors through the forward operator. Those models were\nbuilt for simpler classification and regression problems, and earlier attempts to interpret them missed one point — the\nspatial and temporal filters adapt at the same time, and each changes what the other sees. Petrosyan et al. [4] corrected\nthis and derived how to map jointly trained weights onto source locations and their dynamics. The same front end later\ndrove a decoder for invasive ECoG and sEEG speech decoding [5]. Its advantage over post-hoc explanation is that the\ninterpretation refers to the forward model of the MEG signal, not to latent features and a heuristic for ranking them. We\nextend it here to whole-head MEG from many subjects during a demanding language task.\nWe start from Défossez et al. [1], who paired a spatial-attention layer with subject-specific spatial layers so that spatial\nfilters could be trained across participants, then passed the result through convolutional layers to approximate wav2vec\n2.0 audio embeddings [2] under a contrastive objective [3]. We change four things. First, their spatial attention uses 2D\nFourier functions over a flattened projection of the sensor array; we use real spherical harmonics instead [6]. MEG\nsensors sit on a roughly spherical helmet, so this is the natural basis for the field they measure. Our ablations show that\nsome attention layer is needed — removing it altogether costs four to five percentage points — and that the spherical\nparameterization is worth about one further point over the planar one, which is larger than the seed-to-seed variability\nreported below. The main argument for it, though, is physical rather than empirical. Second, we add trainable depthwise\n2\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\ntemporal filters after the subject block, so that each branch matches a source in time as well as in space. Third, we cut\nthe latent representation to K = 25 branches, which imposes a small and physiologically plausible bottleneck. Finally,\none change concerns the data rather than the model: we remove ocular and cardiac components before training. These\nartifacts are a plausible route to shortcut learning here, because eye activity tracks linguistic structure and attended\nspeech even without corresponding visual input [31–33], while cardiac dynamics vary with narrative intensity and\nconscious narrative processing [34, 35]. Left in the data, they could supply the same stimulus-locked acoustic or\nlinguistic information that our occlusion analysis later probes.\nOn MEG-MASC [7] the model retrieves the correct three-second segment among 1005 candidates with 39.8% Top-1\nand 70.4% Top-10 accuracy, averaged over six independently trained solutions. The complete MEG decoder contains\n486,619 trainable parameters, approximately 20× fewer than the brain decoder of Défossez et al. [1] (Appendix C).\nThese figures cannot be compared directly with those reported by Défossez et al. [1], whose test segments are aligned\nto word onsets and whose reported preprocessing does not explicitly remove ocular or cardiac components. Within our\nown ablation grid, where the data and test set are held fixed, the K = 270, five-block configuration—the closest tested\nmodel in branch count and decoder depth—is 14.8 times larger than the main model and scores 3.60 percentage points\nlower in Top-1 accuracy (Appendix C).\nWe first ask where in cortex the information supporting retrieval arises, by mapping the trained spatial and temporal\nweights to source space. We then ask which properties of the speech stream that information corresponds to, by\nintervening on the decoder’s input with paired MEG substitutions in which feature-marked intervals are replaced by\nmatched real-MEG donors. The Methods develop the interpretation framework and the substitution procedure. The\nResults report the recovered cortical sources, the stimulus features the decoder relies on, and a set of compression and\nablation experiments that bound both.\nWith this we hope to demonstrate that an architecture equipped with explicit physical and physiological priors loses no\naccuracy and can still be inspected once trained. Its weights convert into cortical sources and their dynamics, while\npaired input interventions reveal which stimulus-linked information the trained decoder uses. Decoders built this way\ncan serve as instruments for studying the brain, not only as benchmarks for decoding it.\n\n2\n\nTheoretical background\n\nThis section establishes the theoretical and methodological framework for modeling MEG data and identifying taskrelated neural sources. We first introduce the biophysical generative model of MEG signals and review classical spatial\nfiltering techniques used to estimate source activity from sensor measurements. Following a discussion on the trade-offs\nbetween traditional approaches and modern deep learning methods, we briefly outline the mathematically grounded\nframework of Petrosyan et al. [4] for interpreting deep neural networks with factorized spatial-temporal layers. By\nlinking learned network weights back to the spatial topographies and temporal dynamics of the underlying neural\npopulations, this methodology enables the extraction of physiologically meaningful features during complex decoding\ntasks, which we subsequently apply to the decoding of perceived speech.\n2.1\n\nMEG generative equation\n\nThe main cortical contributors to MEG signals are intracellular currents associated with synaptic activity in pyramidal\nneurons. When many similarly oriented pyramidal neurons within a cortical patch are active synchronously, their\ncombined contribution can be approximated by a single equivalent current dipole (ECD) [12, 36] with location vector\nrn = [xn , yn , zn ]⊤ and orientation θ n = [θnx , θny , θnz ]⊤ , where n indexes the neuronal source. The time-varying\namplitude of the n-th ECD is denoted by sn (t).\nThe array of M MEG sensors surrounding the head and located at positions rm , m = 1, . . . , M , samples at each time\ninstant t a vector x(t) = [x1 (t), . . . , xM (t)]⊤ of the weak magnetic field produced by the superposition of the magnetic\nfields generated by the activation moments s(t) = [s1 (t), . . . , sN (t)]⊤ , where N is the number of task-related neuronal\nsources.\nThe signal generated by a single n-th source can be modeled as x(t) = gn sn (t), where gn = g(rn , θ n ) is the\nM × 1 gain vector mapping the activity of the unit dipole at rn with orientation θ n to the sensors. Visualizing gn\nby interpolating its values between sensor locations gives the so-called topography of the source. The collection of\ngain vectors for a grid of candidate cortical locations and orientations is obtained by solving Maxwell’s equations for\nthe head as a volume conductor on the basis of the geometric information about MEG sensors and cortical sources\nextracted from MRI using standard tools [37].\n3\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\nWhen multiple sources are active, linearity of Maxwell’s equations gives\nx(t) =\n\nN\nX\n\ngn sn (t) +\n\nn=1\n\nNu\nX\n\nqu pu (t) + n(t),\n\n(1)\n\nu=1\n\nThe first summation in (1) corresponds to the task-related sources whose activity via some potentially complex non-linear\nmapping predicts our behavioral variable z(t). The second sum describes the contribution of the Nu task-unrelated\nsources to the measured data x(t). The latter can be regarded as the spatially-correlated brain noise term. The last\nelement in this equation n(t) is the spatially-white sensor noise.\nA typical brain decoding task involves designing an algorithm that operates on the sensor measurements x(t) to\ninfer our target behavioral variable z(t). We assume z(t) depends (possibly non-linearly) only on the task-related\nactivity, i.e. on a subset of source activations si (t), i = 1, . . . , N whose contributions are mediated through the\ncorresponding topographies gi . All information about z(t) available from x(t) is therefore confined to the subspace\nS = span{g1 , . . . , gN } spanned by the topographies gn of these task-related sources. Crucially, since S is in general\nnot orthogonal to the brain-noise subspace I = span{q1 , . . . , qNu }, an effective decoder must not only tune to S but\nalso tune away from I.\n2.2\n\nFrom sensor signals to source activity\n\nThe standard tool used in classical MEG analysis to access the activity of neuronal sources sn (t) is a spatial filter wk⊤\napplied to the channel time series:\nŝk (t) = wk⊤ x(t) =\n\nN\nX\n\nwk⊤ gn sn (t) +\n\nn=1\n\nNu\nX\n\nwk⊤ qu pu (t) + wk⊤ n(t).\n\n(2)\n\nu=1\n\nClearly, the vector wk designed to estimate the activity of the k-th source must, on the one hand, be aligned with the\ncorresponding source topography gk , i.e. wk⊤ gn > 0 for k = n. On the other hand, it must be tuned away from the\ntopographies of all other sources, both task-related and task-unrelated, so that their contributions do not leak into the\nestimate of the k-th source activity, i.e. wk⊤ gn ≈ 0 for k ̸= n and wk⊤ qu ≈ 0 for u = 1, . . . , Nu . Given the limited\nnumber of degrees of freedom and the inherently limited rank of multichannel EEG or MEG measurements, we are\ntypically faced with a trade-off between rejecting interference and tuning to the target source.\nUsing the orthogonality principle of estimation theory [38], it can be shown that, when the additive noise and taskunrelated activity are uncorrelated with the task-related signals, the optimal filter matrix W = [w1 , w2 , . . . , wN ] tuned\nto the sources with topographies G = [g1 , g2 , . . . , gN ] and accounting for the spatial structure of the interference in\nx(t) can be expressed as\nW = R−1\nx GRs ,\n\n(3)\n\nwhere Rx = E x(t)x(t)⊤ is the M × M data covariance matrix and Rs is the N × N covariance matrix of the\nsource signals sn (t), n = 1, . . . , N .\n\b\n\nIn practice, we don’t know the pivotal sources and their topographies and it is the goal of a typical neuroscientific study\nto find them. We are often interested in sources whose activity predicts a specific behavioral variable, such as limb\nkinematics or a perceived or produced audio signal, or reflects differences between experimental conditions. In doing\nso we can opt for three main strategies: exhaustive search, optimal filtering, or deep learning. The exhaustive search\napproach relies on the rows of an EEG or MEG inverse operator to define spatial filters W. The primary advantage of\nthis method is its high interpretability, as the resulting filters correspond directly to specific neural source locations.\nHowever, this approach requires a highly accurate inverse operator, suffers from low statistical sensitivity due to the\nmultiple comparisons problem, and is generally not designed to handle complex decoding tasks but rather to perform\nbinary contrasts of several experimental conditions.\nTo circumvent the computational burden of the exhaustive search strategy, optimal spatial filtering provides a task-driven\nalternative. In this framework, spatial filters are learned directly from the data according to method-specific objectives\n[39–41], making the process highly efficient — source localization via an inverse operator is only required as an\noptional final step. Nevertheless, this approach is still not well suited to complex decoding tasks. By constraining the\nbehavioral variable to be a simple function of the filter output or its envelope, it lacks the expressivity required to model\nintricate, non-linear mappings between neural activity and behavior.\nFinally, deep learning represents a fully adaptive, data-driven paradigm where the spatial filters are learned from the\ndata as a part of a more general decoder tuned to solve a classification or regression task. This approach is suited to\n4\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\nFigure 1: Interpretable front-end made as a collection of branches, the k-th branch is highlighted in blue. During\ntraining each branch gets matched to a neural source with specific spatial and dynamical properties.\ncomplex tasks as it connects source activity estimates to the behavioral variable via a complex learnable transformation.\nHowever, these powerful capabilities come with significant trade-offs: deep learning models require massive amounts\nof training data and act somewhat like a \"black box,\" making it notoriously difficult to link the learned features back to\nspecific anatomical sources and their underlying dynamic properties.\nThis shortcoming can be overcome by using an interpretable, physiologically grounded front end that allows us to relate\nthe parameters of the trained network to the neural sources whose activity is pivotal for solving the decoding task.\n2.3\n\nInterpretable neural architectures with factorized spatial-temporal layers\n\nSeveral compact deep neural network architectures have been developed to decode EEG and MEG activity by employing\nfactorized spatial and temporal filtering operations. Notable examples include EEGNet [28], DeepConvNet and its\ncompact variant ShallowConvNet [29], the separable CNN proposed by Waytowich et al. [42], as well as LF-CNN and\nVAR-CNN [30].\nIt is noteworthy that all these architectures contain not only the trainable spatial filters considered above but also the\ntemporal filters that can be tuned to specific dynamical properties of the activity of neuronal sources. While these\nmodels successfully leverage separated spatial and temporal processing stages to perform automatic feature extraction\nwith a minimal number of parameters, none of these earlier studies provided a rigorous interpretation of the learned\nweights. Specifically, the previous interpretation attempts overlooked the fact that in such factorized architectures, both\nthe spatial and temporal filters adapt concurrently during the training procedure.\nTo address this limitation, Petrosyan et al. [4] introduced a theoretically justified framework that provides the proper\nrecipe for interpreting these weights, demonstrating that accurate reconstruction of the underlying neuronal sources\nand their dynamic properties requires accounting for the mutual dependence of the spatial and temporal filters. The\ndescribed modification is apparent if one views the front-end that comprises factorized spatial and temporal filters as a\ncollection of branches where each one adapts to (gets matched to) a particular neural source with specific spatial and\ndynamical properties, see Figure 1. The k-th branch performs the following operation\n\u0001\nrk (t) = f wk⊤ x(t) ∗ hk (t) ,\n\n(4)\n\nwhere ∗ denotes 1D temporal convolution, hk (t) is a T -tap impulse response of the k-th branch temporal filter and f (·)\nis an optional element-wise non-linearity.\nApplying equation (3) to the branch operation equation (4), taking into account the fact that the spatial and temporal\nprocessing occur within a mutual context and stating that Rs is unknown in practice, we can arrive at the following\nrecipe for deriving the corresponding spatial and temporal patterns from the learned filter weights.\nThe estimate of the spatial pattern of the k-th pivotal source is computed as\nĝk ∝ Rhk wk ,\n⊤\n\n(5)\n\nwhere Rhk = E{(x(t).∗ hk (τ ))(x(t).∗ hk (τ )) } is the covariance of the multichannel data filtered with the temporal\nfilter of the k-th branch and .∗ denotes per-channel convolution. The intuition here is that the spatial filter, while\n5\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\ntuning away from the interfering sources, takes into account the extent to which the contribution of these interfering\nsources is eliminated by the temporal filter operating within the same branch and therefore uses the accordingly filtered\nmultichannel data to compute the spatial covariance matrix.\nTo map the branch-specific topographies ĝk onto the cortex, one can choose from a variety of inverse-modeling\napproaches [43]. In this work, we used the Minimum Norm Estimate (MNE) [44]. In its basic form, the corresponding\nlinear inverse operator can be written as\n⊤\n2\nWMNE = G⊤\nall Gall Gall + λ I\n\n\u0001−1\n\n,\n\n(6)\n\nwhere Gall ∈ RM ×P contains the sensor topographies of all source components included in the forward model, and P\ndepends on the cortical source space and orientation model. The specific noise covariance, depth weighting, orientation\nconstraint, and regularization used for the reported cortical maps are described in Section 3.4.\nAnalysis of each branch also yields a temporal pattern describing the second-order temporal structure selected by that\nbranch. Let ak (t) = wk⊤ x(t) + bk be the spatially filtered branch signal and let ak (t) = [ak (t), ak (t − 1), . . . , ak (t −\nT + 1)]⊤ denote its lag-embedded representation. Using an ordering of the temporal-filter coefficients consistent with\nthis lag vector, the temporal pattern is computed as\np̂k ∝ Rak hk ,\n\nRak = Cov[ak (t)] .\n\n(7)\n\nIn the reported analyses, Rak was estimated with Ledoit–Wolf shrinkage and T = 15. The magnitude spectrum\n|F{p̂k }| was used as a descriptive frequency-domain representation of this temporal pattern. Thus, each branch is\ncharacterized by a spatial pattern ĝk and a temporal pattern p̂k derived while accounting for the covariance structure of\nthe corresponding filtered signals.\nAlthough the number K of effective branches representing the neuronal sources necessary for decoding is typically\nunknown a priori, it can be inferred iteratively. Specifically, K is identified as the minimum number of branches\nrequired before decoding performance plateaus or begins to degrade. Following Petrosyan et al. [4], we can treat K\nas the estimate of N in equation (1). Once K is established, we can estimate the task-informative signal subspace\nas the K-dimensional subspace Ŝ = span{ĝ1 , . . . , ĝK } within the M -dimensional measurement space. This is the\nsubspace the trained network restricts its attention to in order to solve the posed decoding task. Similarly to the\nindividual topographies forming this subspace, the signal subspace as a whole can be scrutinized in the source space\nusing subspace matching approaches such as RAP-MUSIC [45] or its later versions.\nHistorically, the majority of interpretable, compact deep neural network architectures utilizing factorized spatialtemporal filtering—such as those proposed by Lawhern et al. [28], DeepConvNet [29], separable CNNs [42], and\nLF-CNN/VAR-CNN [30]—have been applied to relatively simple classification or regression tasks. These models\ntypically lacked sophisticated decoder heads beyond their factorized blocks; at most, architectures like DeepConvNet\nemployed a simple succession of convolutional layers. However, as demonstrated by Petrosyan et al. [5], such\ninterpretable front-ends can be successfully adapted for highly complex tasks like overt speech decoding. In their\napproach, a recurrent neural network processed the outputs from the spatial-temporal filtering block to predict speech\nmel-spectrograms from ongoing brain activity immediately preceding an utterance. Subsequent localization of the pivotal\nneural sources—derived from the individual branches of the network’s front-end—successfully identified languagerelated cortical sites whose locations showed correspondence with regions where direct electrical stimulation induced\nspeech arrest and production errors, supporting the physiological relevance of the extracted features. Furthermore, the\nanalysis of temporal patterns revealed a relationship between compact spatial patterns and high central frequencies.\nThis relationship is highly characteristic of true neural activity, confirming that the model’s predictions were driven by\ngenuine brain signals rather than being confounded by muscular artifacts.\nIn the current work we apply the methodology of Petrosyan et al. [4] to the task of decoding perceived speech from the\nconcurrently recorded MEG data.\n\n3\n\nMethods\n\nAs stated earlier, in this work we solve the retrieval task in which the network learns to construct embeddings for MEG\ndata similar to the audio embeddings produced by wav2vec 2.0 [2], with a CLIP-style objective [3], following [1].\nOur architecture, see Figure 2 inherits the overall block structure of [1] but replaces its spatial-attention layer with a\nphysically motivated one, augments it with a temporal-filtering layer in the spirit of [4, 5] and modifies a convolutional\ndecoder.\n6\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\nInterpretable Front-End\nMEG data\nM = 208\nchannels,\n3 seconds\n\nConvolutional Module\nTemporal\nFiltering (Linear)","numLines":400,"startLine":1,"totalLines":3131}}}
