{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-af32ae3cc534028e","type":"tool_result","content":"400\tFiltering (Linear)\n401\t\n402\tSpatial Filtering (Affine)\n403\t1\n404\t\n405\t3D Spatial\n406\tAttention\n407\t\n408\tSphericalHarmonic\n409\tAttention Matrix:\n410\t\n411\t2\n412\t\n413\tUnmixing\n414\t1×1 Conv\n415\t\n416\tChannel Mixing\n417\t(Projection) Matrix:\n418\t\n419\t3\n420\t\n421\tSubject\n422\tLayer\n423\t\n424\tS Subject-Specific\n425\tMatrices:\n426\t\n427\tSubject 1\n428\t\n429\tSubject 2\n430\t\n431\tSubject s\n432\t\n433\tSubject S\n434\t\n435\tFiltered\n436\tper-branch\n437\tsignals\n438\t\n439\tB Convolutional Blocks\n440\t(\n441\t\n442\t+\n443\t\n444\tDepthwise temporal\n445\tconvolution per branch\n446\t\n447\t...\n448\t...\n449\t\n450\tConvolutional\n451\tHead\n452\t\n453\tMEG\n454\tembedding\n455\t\n456\t...\n457\t\n458\t+\n459\t150 ms kernel\n460\t\n461\tSubject\n462\tID\n463\t\n464\tFigure 2: Our network’s architecture for MEG-to-audio embedding alignment. A 3-second, 208-channel MEG segment\n465\tis processed by an interpretable front end: spherical-harmonic 3D spatial attention maps the sensor signals to 270\n466\tgeometry-constrained virtual channels; a 1 × 1 unmixing convolution applies a learned affine transformation in this\n467\tchannel space; the subject-specific layer then projects the representation to K interpretable branches selected by the\n468\tsubject ID. Each branch is passed through a depthwise temporal convolution with a 150 ms kernel, producing filtered\n469\tbranch-wise signals. These signals are processed by a convolutional module with B residual convolutional blocks,\n470\twhere we evaluate B ∈ {0, . . . , 5} and use B = 2 in the main architecture, followed by a convolutional head that\n471\toutputs the MEG embedding aligned with the wav2vec audio embedding.\n472\t3.1\n473\t\n474\t3D spatial attention layer\n475\t\n476\tDéfossez et al. [1] parameterized the spatial-attention layer through 2-D Fourier functions defined over a planar\n477\tprojection of the sensor layout. Because the MEG sensors occupy a three-dimensional, approximately spherical\n478\tarrangement, we instead parameterize each of the J = 270 virtual channels by real spherical harmonics. For virtual\n479\tchannel j and sensor m, the unnormalized coefficient is\n480\tcjm =\n481\t\n482\tL−1\n483\tℓ\n484\tX X\n485\t\n486\tγjq,ℓ Yℓq (θm , φm ),\n487\t\n488\t(8)\n489\t\n490\tℓ=0 q=−ℓ\n491\t\n492\twhere (θm , φm ) are the polar and azimuthal angles of sensor m, Yℓq is a real spherical-harmonic basis function, and\n493\tγjq,ℓ is learned. We set L = 24, so the implementation uses degrees ℓ = 0, . . . , 23 and therefore L2 = 576 basis\n494\tfunctions for each virtual channel. The coefficients are normalized across the M sensors separately for each virtual\n495\tchannel, e\n496\tcj = softmax(cj ), and applied as\n497\tSAj (x(t)) = e\n498\tc⊤\n499\tj x(t),\n500\t\n501\tj = 1, . . . , J.\n502\t\n503\t(9)\n504\t\n505\tThe coefficients are fixed for a trained model and do not depend on the current input sample; the term “spatial attention”\n506\tis retained for consistency with [1].\n507\t3.2\n508\t\n509\tInterpretable front-end\n510\t\n511\tThe interpretable subject block follows the factorized spatial–temporal structure introduced in Eq. (4). Parameter\n512\tK controls the number of branches in the block. We expect each branch to tune, during training, to a particular\n513\tneuronal source active in a specific frequency range and characterized by a defined topography. For participant s, let\n514\tC ∈ R270×208 denote the softmax-normalized 3-D attention matrix, Wu ∈ R270×270 and bu ∈ R270 the weights and\n515\tbias of the shared 1 × 1 unmixing convolution, and Ws ∈ RK×270 the participant-specific projection. The effective\n516\tparticipant-specific spatial filtering matrix is\n517\tW(s) = Ws Wu C ∈ RK×208 ,\n518\t\n519\t(10)\n520\t\n521\tb(s) = Ws bu ∈ RK .\n522\t\n523\t(11)\n524\t\n525\tand the corresponding branch-wise bias is\n526\t\n527\t7\n528\t\n529\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n530\t\n531\tThe branch signals before temporal filtering are therefore\n532\tas (t) = W(s) xs (t) + b(s) .\n533\t\n534\t(12)\n535\t\n536\t(s)\n537\t⊤\n538\tThe k-th row ws,k\n539\tof W(s) defines the spatial filter used by branch k, while bk is its learned offset.\n540\t\n541\tBrain rhythms are key components of non-invasively measured electrical neuronal activity, reflecting different aspects\n542\tof how the cortex processes incoming information [46]. To target specific frequency ranges, we applied one trainable\n543\t1-D depthwise temporal filter to each of the K branch signals. Each filter had 15 samples, corresponding to 150 ms at\n544\tthe MEG sampling rate of fs = 100 Hz. The layer used same-length zero padding, K groups, and no bias term. The\n545\ttemporal filters were shared across participants, whereas the preceding spatial projection was participant-specific. No\n546\tgating or pointwise non-linearity was applied after this layer. Therefore, the output of branch k is then obtained by\n547\tapplying its temporal filter to as,k (t):\n548\t\u0001\n549\trs,k (t) = as,k ∗ hk (t).\n550\t(13)\n551\t3.3\n552\t\n553\tNon-linear decoder\n554\t\n555\tThe K branch-wise signals produced by the interpretable front end were passed to a non-linear temporal decoder\n556\tadapted from the convolutional brain decoder of Défossez et al. [1]. The decoder comprised B temporal convolutional\n557\tblocks followed by a convolutional head. We evaluated B ∈ {0, . . . , 5}; when B = 0, the branch signals were passed\n558\tdirectly to the convolutional head.\n559\tEach temporal block contained three one-dimensional convolutions with kernel size 3 and stride 1. For block b =\n560\t0, . . . , B − 1, the dilation factors of the first two convolutions were\n561\tdb,1 = 2(2b mod 5) ,\n562\t\n563\tdb,2 = 2((2b+1) mod 5) .\n564\t\n565\t(14)\n566\t\n567\tBoth convolutions preserved the temporal length through padding and were followed by batch normalization and a\n568\tGELU activation. The second convolution used a residual connection in every block, while the first convolution used a\n569\tresidual connection in all blocks except the first. The third convolution used kernel size 3, stride 1, and dilation 2, and\n570\tincreased the channel dimension from K to 2K. A gated linear unit then reduced it back to K channels.\n571\tThe convolutional head first applied a GELU activation and then projected the K decoder channels to the F = 768\n572\twav2vec feature channels using a one-dimensional convolution with kernel size 3, stride 2, and no padding, followed by\n573\tbatch normalization. Consequently, an input containing T MEG samples produced\n574\t\u0016\n575\t\u0017\n576\tT −3\n577\tT′ =\n578\t+1\n579\t(15)\n580\t2\n581\toutput time points. For the 3 s inputs sampled at 100 Hz used in the main experiment, this transformed the K × 300\n582\tbranch representation into a 768 × 149 MEG-derived embedding. The main model used K = 25 branches and B = 2\n583\ttemporal convolutional blocks.\n584\t3.4\n585\t\n586\tSpatial weights interpretation and source localization\n587\t\n588\t⊤\n589\tFor participant s, the k-th row ws,k\n590\tof the effective spatial-filter matrix W(s) defined in Eq. (10) is the spatial filter of\n591\tbranch k. We compute the corresponding spatial pattern ĝs,k using Eq. (5).\n592\t\n593\tFor the interpretation analyses to avoid DC component, each learned temporal kernel was replaced by its zero-mean\n594\tversion\n595\t15\n596\t1 X\n597\te\n598\thk (τ ) = hk (τ ) −\n599\thk (τ ′ ).\n600\t(16)\n601\t15 ′\n602\tτ =1\n603\t\n604\tThis transformation was applied only when computing the covariance-based spatial patterns, temporal patterns, spectra,\n605\tsource estimates, SVD, and clusters. Training, checkpoint selection, retrieval evaluation, and the paired MEG occlusion\n606\tanalysis used the original learned kernels hk . Accordingly, e\n607\thk rather than hk is used in the interpretation results reported\n608\tbelow. The covariance matrix in Eq. (5) was estimated with Ledoit–Wolf shrinkage after filtering the MEG data with\n609\te\n610\thk .\n611\tFor cortical localization, we applied the MNE inverse solution using MNE-Python [47] to the branch-specific sensor\n612\ttopographies. The forward model was constructed on the fsaverage cortical template using an ico4 source space,\n613\ta boundary-element head model, and a common MEG-to-template coregistration. We used an ad hoc sensor-noise\n614\tcovariance, a loose orientation constraint of 0.5, depth weighting of 0.5, and a regularization parameter of λ2 = 1/3.\n615\t8\n616\t\n617\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n618\t\n619\tFor each branch, we retained the absolute source amplitudes and normalized the resulting cortical map to unit L2\n620\tnorm before visualisation and clustering. Because all participants were mapped using the same template anatomy\n621\tand coregistration, the resulting maps represent common-template source estimates rather than participant-specific\n622\tanatomical localizations.\n623\t3.5\n624\t\n625\tClustering branch patterns across participants\n626\t\n627\tFor the participant–branch analysis, we computed the spatial and temporal patterns described in Section 2.3 for each\n628\tof the K = 25 branches and all 27 participants, using the Cable Spool Fort recordings from the first session. Before\n629\tcomputing these patterns, we subtracted the mean from each learned temporal kernel to remove its DC component.\n630\tThe sensor-space spatial pattern was projected onto the common cortical source space; we retained the absolute source\n631\tamplitudes and normalized each resulting map to unit L2 norm. The temporal representation was the 15-sample pattern\n632\tof Eq. (7). Its FFT magnitude was used for visualisation, whereas its log-magnitude spectrum was used to compare\n633\tbranches. This produced 27 × 25 = 675 participant–branch items, each represented by a cortical source-magnitude\n634\tmap and a temporal spectrum.\n635\tsrc\n636\tFor items i and j, we computed the Pearson correlation rij\n637\tbetween their cortical source-magnitude maps and the\n638\ttemp\n639\tPearson correlation rij between their log-magnitude spectra. We combined the two views through the distance\n640\t(\n641\t)\n642\ttemp\n643\tsrc\n644\t+1\n645\trij\n646\t+ 1 rij\n647\tdij = 1 − min\n648\t,\n649\t.\n650\t(17)\n651\t2\n652\t2\n653\t\n654\tThis weakest-view combination makes two branches close only when both their cortical distributions and their temporal\n655\tfrequency profiles are similar.\n656\tBefore clustering, we screened the sensor-space spatial patterns for excessive local roughness. Roughness was defined\n657\tas the mean squared difference between each sensor value and the values at its six nearest sensors, divided by the\n658\tvariance of the pattern across sensors. Patterns with roughness greater than 1 were excluded from clustering.\n659\tWe applied complete-linkage agglomerative clustering to all remaining participant–branch items and cut the hierarchy\n660\tat a minimum raw Pearson correlation of τ = 0.25 in both views, corresponding to a distance threshold of (1 − τ )/2 =\n661\t0.375 in Eq. (17). Taking the minimum across views makes the pairwise criterion strict: two branches can be close only\n662\tif both their source maps and temporal spectra are similar. Complete linkage then makes the cluster-level criterion strict\n663\tby enforcing this bound for the least similar pair in a cluster. Because a single low-similarity pair can therefore block\n664\ta merge, we used τ = 0.25 as a deliberately permissive worst-case bound rather than as the expected within-cluster\n665\tsimilarity. Consequently, every pair within a resulting cluster had both raw correlations of at least 0.25.\n666\tEach cluster was represented by its medoid: the branch with the highest mean weakest-view similarity to the other\n667\tmembers of that cluster.\n668\t3.6\n669\t\n670\tPaired MEG occlusion analysis of stimulus-feature use\n671\t\n672\tTo determine which stimulus-linked neural information supports the trained decoder’s retrieval decisions, we tested\n673\ta battery of candidate features spanning broad acoustic state, rapid spectral change, phonetic identity, lexical timing,\n674\tcontextual predictability, and experimentally inserted disruptions of the narrative. The analysis contrasts matched MEG\n675\tsubstitutions associated with the presence and absence of each feature. We therefore first define the candidate features\n676\tand their control states before describing the substitution procedure.\n677\tStimulus features. We evaluated 19 acoustic, phonetic, and linguistic features on the same 100 Hz audio timeline used\n678\tto construct the MEG–audio pairs. Four binary features described complete silent periods, silence onsets corresponding\n679\tto speech cessation, silence offsets corresponding to speech resumption, and the union of the two boundary masks.\n680\tSeven further features marked vowels, stops, fricatives, sibilants, nasals, liquids/glides, and schwa using Montreal\n681\tForced Aligner TextGrid annotations. These phoneme groups were not mutually exclusive: sibilants were also included\n682\tamong fricatives, and schwa among vowels.\n683\tA separate word-onset feature marked the beginning of every spoken word, including words occurring within continuous\n684\tspeech rather than only after silence. Two features captured the dataset’s controlled departures from natural narrative\n685\tspeech. Pseudowords were non-words inserted within otherwise coherent sentences, providing spoken sequences\n686\twithout an associated lexical meaning. Random word lists consisted of words taken from the preceding approximately\n687\tfive minutes of the story and presented in random order. This preserved individual words while removing normal\n688\tsentence and narrative structure. The corresponding masks covered the full spoken duration of each pseudoword or\n689\tword-list segment.\n690\t9\n691\t\n692\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n693\t\n694\tThe continuous linguistic features were word surprisal, predictive entropy, and word frequency. Surprisal was computed\n695\twith GPT-2 as the summed negative log2 probability of the subword tokens forming each word. Predictive entropy was\n696\tthe entropy of the model’s next-token distribution immediately before the word, and frequency was measured on the\n697\tZipf scale. The remaining continuous features described basic acoustic structure. Loudness was an energy-based proxy\n698\tcomputed as the log mean-square waveform energy in non-overlapping 10 ms frames. Acoustic-onset strength was a\n699\tspectral-flux envelope computed from a centred log-power mel spectrogram using a 128 ms analysis window and a\n700\t10 ms hop, and therefore measured rapid changes in the stimulus spectrum.\n701\tFeature states and controls. For the binary features, the annotated intervals constituted the feature-present state. The\n702\tcontinuous features were converted into binary contrasts. For surprisal, predictive entropy, loudness, and acoustic-onset\n703\tstrength, the upper and lower quartiles defined the feature-present and feature-absent states, respectively. For word\n704\tfrequency, the direction was reversed so that rare and frequent words formed the feature-present and feature-absent\n705\tstates. Quartile thresholds for the word-level features were estimated from ordinary narrative words in the five non-test\n706\tpieces of Black Willow; pseudowords and words presented in the random word-list segments were excluded from\n707\tthreshold estimation.\n708\tThe feature-absent state was chosen to provide a meaningful control rather than a generic complement whenever\n709\tpossible. For each phoneme class, the control comprised other labelled phonemes. For pseudoword and random\n710\tword-list insertions, it comprised unmanipulated narrative words. For the remaining binary features, it comprised time\n711\toutside the marked feature intervals. Feature-present masks were expanded by 20 ms on either side to accommodate\n712\tsmall timing errors. When a predefined control mask was available, it was expanded likewise and any resulting overlap\n713\twith the feature-present mask was removed. Otherwise, the feature-absent donor pool was defined as all time outside\n714\tthe expanded feature-present mask.\n715\tPaired MEG substitution. The 1005 test queries were the 3 s windows from the final seven pieces of Black Willow.\n716\tReal-MEG donor intervals were drawn from the five non-test pieces of the same story. For every participant and session,\n717\twe selected test windows that contained the feature but were not completely covered by it. All feature-positive intervals\n718\twithin an eligible query were replaced simultaneously in two conditions:\n719\tX f →0 : replacement by MEG from a feature-absent interval,\n720\tX\n721\t\n722\tf →f\n723\t\n724\t: replacement by MEG from a feature-present interval.\n725\t\n726\t(18)\n727\t(19)\n728\t\n729\tThe feature-present substitution served as a matched control for the replacement procedure itself. Comparing X f →0\n730\tdirectly with the unmodified query would conflate the effect of changing the feature state with generic consequences of\n731\treplacing the original MEG, including loss of query-specific activity, donor mismatch, and blending at the replacement\n732\tboundaries. In contrast, both substitution arms replaced the same intervals with real donor MEG under the same\n733\tmatching and tapering procedure, while differing in whether the donor interval was associated with the feature. The\n734\tunmodified-query ranks were retained as a descriptive baseline for quantifying the total retrieval damage produced by\n735\teach substitution arm, but were not used as the primary feature-specific contrast.\n736\tDonor intervals had exactly the same duration as the replaced interval and came from the same participant and session as\n737\tthe target query. Within each matched pair, the feature-present and feature-absent donors came from the same audio file;\n738\tthe absent interval was selected as the nearest available match to a randomly sampled present interval. All 208 channels\n739\twere copied together, and a 20 ms raised-cosine taper blended each replacement into the original signal. Because\n740\tmultiple valid donor intervals were available, an effect estimated from a single donor pair could depend substantially\n741\ton that particular random selection. We therefore evaluated each eligible query using five matched donor pairs and\n742\taveraged over these repetitions in the rank contrast below, reducing variability attributable to donor selection. These\n743\trepetitions were used for averaging and were not treated as independent observations. The fixed 150 ms MEG–audio\n744\tlatency used during training and testing was retained when donor intervals were extracted.\n745\tImportantly, windows without the feature, windows fully covered by the feature, and windows for which a complete set\n746\tof matched donor pairs was unavailable were excluded only from the feature-specific query average. They remained in\n747\tthe unchanged 1005-candidate retrieval bank for every rank calculation.\n748\tf →0\n749\tf →f\n750\tRank contrast. For eligible query i and donor pair j ∈ {1, . . . , 5}, let rsqij\n751\tand rsqij\n752\tdenote the rank of the correct\n753\taudio candidate for participant s and session q. Rank was defined as one plus the number of candidates with a cosine\n754\tsimilarity strictly greater than that of the correct candidate. The subject–session effect for feature f was\n755\t5\n756\t\n757\tEsqf =\n758\t\n759\t\u0011\n760\t1 X X \u0010 f →0\n761\tf →f\n762\trsqij − rsqij\n763\t,\n764\t5|If |\n765\tj=1\n766\ti∈If\n767\t\n768\t10\n769\t\n770\t(20)\n771\t\n772\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n773\t\n774\twhere If is the set of eligible test queries. Positive values indicate that replacing the marked intervals with feature-absent\n775\tMEG worsened retrieval more than the matched feature-present control. For participants with two sessions, the two\n776\tsession effects were averaged so that every participant contributed one value,\n777\tQs\n778\t\n779\t1 X\n780\tDsf =\n781\tEsqf .\n782\tQs q=1\n783\tStatistical inference.\n784\t\n785\t(21)\n786\t\n787\tFor each feature, the observed statistic was the studentized participant mean,\n788\tTf =\n789\t\n790\tDf\n791\t√ ,\n792\tSD(D1f , . . . , DSf )/ S\n793\t\n794\tS = 27.\n795\t\n796\t(22)\n797\t\n798\tWe tested the directional alternative E[Dsf ] > 0 using Nperm = 100,000 participant sign-flip permutations. In each\n799\tpermutation, one random sign was applied to the complete 19-feature vector of each participant, thereby preserving\n800\tdependencies among features within participants. Familywise error was controlled with a one-sided single-step max-T\n801\t(b)\n802\t(b)\n803\tcorrection. If Tf is the permuted statistic and M (b) = maxg Tg , the corrected value was\n804\t\u0003\n805\tPNperm \u0002 (b)\n806\t1 + b=1\n807\t1 M ≥ Tf\n808\tFWER\n809\tpf\n810\t=\n811\t.\n812\t(23)\n813\tNperm + 1\n814\tFeature-present control and significance criterion. A difference between the two substitution conditions is interpretable only if the feature-present substitution preserves meaningful retrieval. If replacing the marked intervals with\n815\tfeature-present MEG already reduces retrieval to random ranking, the contrast with the feature-absent substitution may\n816\treflect different degrees of generic disruption rather than the decoder’s use of the tested feature. We therefore used the\n817\tf → f condition as a validity check for each feature.\n818\tAfter averaging donor pairs and query windows within each participant–session and then sessions within each participant,\n819\twe computed the mean f → f rank across the 27 participants. Under random ordering of 1005 candidates, the correct\n820\ttarget is equally likely to occupy any rank from 1 to 1005, so the expected mean rank is\n821\t1 + 1005\n822\t= 503.\n823\t(24)\n824\t2\n825\tThe feature-present control was considered valid when its mean rank was below 503. This check was performed once\n826\tper feature and was not used to exclude individual query windows.\n827\tE[rrandom ] =\n828\t\n829\tTherefore, a feature was marked as showing a significant positive occlusion effect only when all three criteria were\n830\tmet: the mean participant-level contrast Df was positive, the one-sided participant sign-flip max-T test yielded\n831\tpFWER\n832\t< 0.05, and the mean rank in the f → f condition was below 503.\n833\tf\n834\t3.7\n835\t\n836\tDataset and preprocessing\n837\t\n838\tWe use the MEG-MASC dataset [7], comprising audio and MEG signals from 49 approximately one-hour participant–\n839\tsession recordings obtained from 27 English-speaking participants. Twenty-two participants contributed two sessions\n840\tand five contributed one. In each available session, participants listened to four fictional stories from the MASC corpus.\n841\tThe study was approved by the institutional review board ethics committee of New York University Abu Dhabi [7].\n842\tAudio was resampled to 16 kHz and divided into 3 s windows with a 1 s stride. We discarded windows whose peak\n843\tabsolute waveform amplitude was below 10−4 . A window was retained only if at least 50% of the duration of one or\n844\tmore annotated words fell within it. The target representation for each retained window was obtained with the wav2vec\n845\t2.0 Base model [2] by averaging its final four hidden layers at each model time step.\n846\tOcular and cardiac ICA components were removed from the MEG recordings before further preprocessing. The data\n847\twere resampled from 1000 to 100 Hz. Separately for each participant–session–story recording, we subtracted the\n848\tper-channel mean over the 0.5 s immediately preceding the first stimulus onset, robust-scaled each channel using its\n849\tmedian and interquartile range, standardized it to zero mean and unit variance, and clipped the resulting values to ±20\n850\tstandard deviations.\n851\tThe entire stories LW1, Cable Spool Fort, and Easy Money, together with the first five pieces of Black Willow, formed a\n852\t2698-segment development set. Within this set, the fifth piece of Black Willow was held out for validation and excluded\n853\tfrom neural-network parameter fitting. The last seven pieces of Black Willow formed the test set of 1005 candidate\n854\t11\n855\t\n856\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n857\t\n858\tsegments. For Black Willow, the scaling parameters were fitted only on samples preceding the first test-piece onset to\n859\tavoid test leakage. Each 3 s audio segment was paired with the 3 s MEG segment beginning 150 ms later to account for\n860\tauditory-response latency, similarly to [1]. In contrast to [1], we did not align test segments to word onsets, resulting in\n861\ta less structured and therefore more challenging retrieval setting.\n862\t3.8\n863\t\n864\tTraining and testing\n865\t\n866\tModels were trained with the AdamW optimizer [48], using a learning rate of 3 × 10−4 for the network parameters and\n867\t10−3 for the learned temperature, zero weight decay, a batch size of 100, and at most 50 epochs. Early stopping used a\n868\tpatience of seven epochs and checkpoint selection was based on the lowest validation loss. Unless stated otherwise, the\n869\treported main checkpoint, architectural sweeps, ablations, and interpretation analyses use seed 42. All experiments used\n870\tthe L = 24 spherical-harmonic setting defined in Section 3.1, corresponding to degrees 0, . . . , 23 and 576 real basis\n871\tfunctions per virtual channel. The training objective was a one-directional MEG-to-audio contrastive cross-entropy loss.\n872\tMEG-derived embeddings were compared with the unique audio targets represented in the current minibatch. Within\n873\teach minibatch, when multiple MEG-derived embeddings corresponded to the same audio-segment target, they were\n874\tall assigned to the same target embedding. The resulting similarity matrix therefore had one row per MEG-derived\n875\tembedding and U columns, where U is the number of unique audio targets in the minibatch. Using this matrix instead\n876\tof a square batch-by-batch similarity matrix prevents duplicate instances of the same positive audio target from being\n877\ttreated as negatives in the cross-entropy loss. Similarities were computed after L2 normalization over the feature–time\n878\tdimensions and divided by a learned temperature parameter. Training was performed on a cluster node with a single\n879\tNVIDIA Tesla V100 32 GB GPU and 8 cores of Intel Xeon Gold 6152 CPU.\n880\tTo estimate the effect of a window size, we also evaluated paired MEG and audio segments of W ∈ {1.5, 2.25, 3, 4, 5} s.\n881\tWe retained the start positions of the original 3 s segments rather than constructing a new set of segments at each\n882\tduration. For an anchor at time t, the audio target was regenerated directly from the continuous recording over [t, t + W ],\n883\tand the corresponding MEG input covered [t + 150 ms, t + W + 150 ms]. Wav2vec 2.0 audio embeddings were then\n884\trecomputed separately for each window size.\n885\tAs longer window sizes usually result in fewer windows, to keep the training-set size and retrieval-database size\n886\tconstant across durations, we used a common set of window starts containing only start positions for which a complete\n887\t5 s segment remained within the audio. This yielded 991 test candidates at every duration. Separate models with\n888\tK = 25 branches and B ∈ {0, 2, 5} convolutional blocks were trained for each duration, with all other training and\n889\tcheckpoint-selection procedures kept unchanged. The 3 s ablation condition was regenerated using this same pipeline\n890\tand common anchor set rather than copied from the main 1005-candidate experiment.\n891\tTo examine the temporal scale required by the interpretable front end, we trained otherwise identical two-block,\n892\tK = 25 models with temporal-filter kernel sizes of 1, 5, 9, 15, 19, 25, 29, and 49 samples, corresponding to supports\n893\tof 10–490 ms at the 100 Hz MEG sampling rate. All conditions used seed 42 and the same data split, optimizer,\n894\tearly-stopping rule, and validation-based checkpoint selection. The one-sample condition contains no temporal context\n895\tand acts only as a learnable scaling of each branch.\n896\t\n897\t4\n898\t\n899\tResults\n900\t\n901\t4.1\n902\t\n903\tDecoding accuracy\n904\t\n905\tWe assess decoding efficacy on the held-out pieces 5–11 of The Black Willow. We first evaluated how retrieval\n906\tperformance depends on two architectural capacity parameters: the number of interpretable branches K and the number\n907\tof convolutional blocks following the network’s front end. For each configuration, we report test Top-1 and Top-10\n908\taccuracy at the checkpoint with the lowest validation loss, so the sweep reflects validation-based model selection rather\n909\tthan test-set checkpoint tuning (Fig. 3).\n910\tBoth metrics show a sharp improvement when K increases from very small values to the intermediate regime. With\n911\tonly one or a few branches, the model is strongly bottlenecked: the branch space is too small to preserve the information\n912\tneeded to distinguish the correct audio segment from the candidate set. Performance then rises rapidly and reaches a\n913\tbroad high-accuracy plateau around K = 10–25. This is the main trend of the sweep: tens of branches are sufficient to\n914\trecover most of the retrieval performance, while increasing K toward hundreds does not produce systematic gains.\n915\tThe behavior at large K is also informative. If the branch dimension were simply acting as generic model capacity, one\n916\twould expect performance to keep improving, or at least remain monotonic, as K grows. Instead, several curves flatten\n917\tand then mildly decline at larger K. This suggests that the useful task-related subspace is compact, and that adding\n918\t12\n919\t\n920\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n921\t\n922\tTop-1 accuracy\n923\t\n924\t0.4\n925\t\n926\t0.3\n927\t\n928\t0.2\n929\t\n930\t0.1\n931\t\n932\t0.6\n933\t0.5\n934\t0.4\n935\tTemporal blocks\n936\t\n937\t5 Blocks\n938\t\n939\t250\n940\t\n941\t4 Blocks\n942\t\n943\t2 Blocks\n944\t\n945\t200\n946\t\n947\t3 Blocks\n948\t\n949\t1 Block\n950\t\n951\t150\n952\t\n953\t50\n954\t\n955\t25\n956\t\n957\t10\n958\t\n959\t5\n960\t\n961\t1\n962\t\n963\t0.2\n964\t\n965\t0 Blocks\n966\t\n967\t100\n968\t\n969\t0.3\n970\t\n971\t75\n972\t\n973\tTop-10 accuracy\n974\t\n975\t0.7\n976\t\n977\tK (unmix channels)\n978\t\n979\tFigure 3: Retrieval accuracy as a function of the number of interpretable branches K and the number of convolutional\n980\tblocks in the decoder. For each configuration, Top-1 and Top-10 test accuracy are reported at the checkpoint with the\n981\tlowest validation loss. Accuracy increases sharply from very small K to approximately K = 10–25, then enters a\n982\tbroad plateau; larger K does not produce systematic gains and can mildly degrade performance. Across decoder depths,\n983\tthe 0-block model is consistently weaker, while models with 2–5 convolutional blocks form a similar high-performing\n984\tregime. The main 2-conv, K = 25 configuration lies on this compact high-accuracy plateau.\n985\t\n986\tmany extra branch channels mostly increases redundant or harder-to-regularize degrees of freedom rather than adding\n987\tnew decodable information.\n988\tConvolutional depth modulates this pattern but does not change the overall conclusion. The model without convolutional\n989\tblocks is consistently weaker once K enters the useful range, showing that the compact branch representation still\n990\tneeds downstream temporal/contextual processing. Adding convolutional blocks substantially improves retrieval, but\n991\tthe gains saturate: models with two or more blocks occupy a similar high-performing regime over the plateau. Deeper\n992\tvariants can match or slightly exceed the two-block model at some values of K, but they do not produce a qualitatively\n993\tdifferent dependence on branch dimensionality.\n994\t4.2\n995\t\n996\tModified attention scores\n997\t\n998\tFigure 4 compares the mean softmax-normalized attention weights across the J = 270 virtual channels,\n999\tJ\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b9c66524-a39d-41df-a83a-b672ae01d3f5","uuid":"e1ff42fa-df5c-43a8-ab93-4920404f6410","timestamp":"2026-08-04T15:54:15.988Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/2608.01481v1.txt","content":"Filtering (Linear)\n\nSpatial Filtering (Affine)\n1\n\n3D Spatial\nAttention\n\nSphericalHarmonic\nAttention Matrix:\n\n2\n\nUnmixing\n1×1 Conv\n\nChannel Mixing\n(Projection) Matrix:\n\n3\n\nSubject\nLayer\n\nS Subject-Specific\nMatrices:\n\nSubject 1\n\nSubject 2\n\nSubject s\n\nSubject S\n\nFiltered\nper-branch\nsignals\n\nB Convolutional Blocks\n(\n\n+\n\nDepthwise temporal\nconvolution per branch\n\n...\n...\n\nConvolutional\nHead\n\nMEG\nembedding\n\n...\n\n+\n150 ms kernel\n\nSubject\nID\n\nFigure 2: Our network’s architecture for MEG-to-audio embedding alignment. A 3-second, 208-channel MEG segment\nis processed by an interpretable front end: spherical-harmonic 3D spatial attention maps the sensor signals to 270\ngeometry-constrained virtual channels; a 1 × 1 unmixing convolution applies a learned affine transformation in this\nchannel space; the subject-specific layer then projects the representation to K interpretable branches selected by the\nsubject ID. Each branch is passed through a depthwise temporal convolution with a 150 ms kernel, producing filtered\nbranch-wise signals. These signals are processed by a convolutional module with B residual convolutional blocks,\nwhere we evaluate B ∈ {0, . . . , 5} and use B = 2 in the main architecture, followed by a convolutional head that\noutputs the MEG embedding aligned with the wav2vec audio embedding.\n3.1\n\n3D spatial attention layer\n\nDéfossez et al. [1] parameterized the spatial-attention layer through 2-D Fourier functions defined over a planar\nprojection of the sensor layout. Because the MEG sensors occupy a three-dimensional, approximately spherical\narrangement, we instead parameterize each of the J = 270 virtual channels by real spherical harmonics. For virtual\nchannel j and sensor m, the unnormalized coefficient is\ncjm =\n\nL−1\nℓ\nX X\n\nγjq,ℓ Yℓq (θm , φm ),\n\n(8)\n\nℓ=0 q=−ℓ\n\nwhere (θm , φm ) are the polar and azimuthal angles of sensor m, Yℓq is a real spherical-harmonic basis function, and\nγjq,ℓ is learned. We set L = 24, so the implementation uses degrees ℓ = 0, . . . , 23 and therefore L2 = 576 basis\nfunctions for each virtual channel. The coefficients are normalized across the M sensors separately for each virtual\nchannel, e\ncj = softmax(cj ), and applied as\nSAj (x(t)) = e\nc⊤\nj x(t),\n\nj = 1, . . . , J.\n\n(9)\n\nThe coefficients are fixed for a trained model and do not depend on the current input sample; the term “spatial attention”\nis retained for consistency with [1].\n3.2\n\nInterpretable front-end\n\nThe interpretable subject block follows the factorized spatial–temporal structure introduced in Eq. (4). Parameter\nK controls the number of branches in the block. We expect each branch to tune, during training, to a particular\nneuronal source active in a specific frequency range and characterized by a defined topography. For participant s, let\nC ∈ R270×208 denote the softmax-normalized 3-D attention matrix, Wu ∈ R270×270 and bu ∈ R270 the weights and\nbias of the shared 1 × 1 unmixing convolution, and Ws ∈ RK×270 the participant-specific projection. The effective\nparticipant-specific spatial filtering matrix is\nW(s) = Ws Wu C ∈ RK×208 ,\n\n(10)\n\nb(s) = Ws bu ∈ RK .\n\n(11)\n\nand the corresponding branch-wise bias is\n\n7\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\nThe branch signals before temporal filtering are therefore\nas (t) = W(s) xs (t) + b(s) .\n\n(12)\n\n(s)\n⊤\nThe k-th row ws,k\nof W(s) defines the spatial filter used by branch k, while bk is its learned offset.\n\nBrain rhythms are key components of non-invasively measured electrical neuronal activity, reflecting different aspects\nof how the cortex processes incoming information [46]. To target specific frequency ranges, we applied one trainable\n1-D depthwise temporal filter to each of the K branch signals. Each filter had 15 samples, corresponding to 150 ms at\nthe MEG sampling rate of fs = 100 Hz. The layer used same-length zero padding, K groups, and no bias term. The\ntemporal filters were shared across participants, whereas the preceding spatial projection was participant-specific. No\ngating or pointwise non-linearity was applied after this layer. Therefore, the output of branch k is then obtained by\napplying its temporal filter to as,k (t):\n\u0001\nrs,k (t) = as,k ∗ hk (t).\n(13)\n3.3\n\nNon-linear decoder\n\nThe K branch-wise signals produced by the interpretable front end were passed to a non-linear temporal decoder\nadapted from the convolutional brain decoder of Défossez et al. [1]. The decoder comprised B temporal convolutional\nblocks followed by a convolutional head. We evaluated B ∈ {0, . . . , 5}; when B = 0, the branch signals were passed\ndirectly to the convolutional head.\nEach temporal block contained three one-dimensional convolutions with kernel size 3 and stride 1. For block b =\n0, . . . , B − 1, the dilation factors of the first two convolutions were\ndb,1 = 2(2b mod 5) ,\n\ndb,2 = 2((2b+1) mod 5) .\n\n(14)\n\nBoth convolutions preserved the temporal length through padding and were followed by batch normalization and a\nGELU activation. The second convolution used a residual connection in every block, while the first convolution used a\nresidual connection in all blocks except the first. The third convolution used kernel size 3, stride 1, and dilation 2, and\nincreased the channel dimension from K to 2K. A gated linear unit then reduced it back to K channels.\nThe convolutional head first applied a GELU activation and then projected the K decoder channels to the F = 768\nwav2vec feature channels using a one-dimensional convolution with kernel size 3, stride 2, and no padding, followed by\nbatch normalization. Consequently, an input containing T MEG samples produced\n\u0016\n\u0017\nT −3\nT′ =\n+1\n(15)\n2\noutput time points. For the 3 s inputs sampled at 100 Hz used in the main experiment, this transformed the K × 300\nbranch representation into a 768 × 149 MEG-derived embedding. The main model used K = 25 branches and B = 2\ntemporal convolutional blocks.\n3.4\n\nSpatial weights interpretation and source localization\n\n⊤\nFor participant s, the k-th row ws,k\nof the effective spatial-filter matrix W(s) defined in Eq. (10) is the spatial filter of\nbranch k. We compute the corresponding spatial pattern ĝs,k using Eq. (5).\n\nFor the interpretation analyses to avoid DC component, each learned temporal kernel was replaced by its zero-mean\nversion\n15\n1 X\ne\nhk (τ ) = hk (τ ) −\nhk (τ ′ ).\n(16)\n15 ′\nτ =1\n\nThis transformation was applied only when computing the covariance-based spatial patterns, temporal patterns, spectra,\nsource estimates, SVD, and clusters. Training, checkpoint selection, retrieval evaluation, and the paired MEG occlusion\nanalysis used the original learned kernels hk . Accordingly, e\nhk rather than hk is used in the interpretation results reported\nbelow. The covariance matrix in Eq. (5) was estimated with Ledoit–Wolf shrinkage after filtering the MEG data with\ne\nhk .\nFor cortical localization, we applied the MNE inverse solution using MNE-Python [47] to the branch-specific sensor\ntopographies. The forward model was constructed on the fsaverage cortical template using an ico4 source space,\na boundary-element head model, and a common MEG-to-template coregistration. We used an ad hoc sensor-noise\ncovariance, a loose orientation constraint of 0.5, depth weighting of 0.5, and a regularization parameter of λ2 = 1/3.\n8\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\nFor each branch, we retained the absolute source amplitudes and normalized the resulting cortical map to unit L2\nnorm before visualisation and clustering. Because all participants were mapped using the same template anatomy\nand coregistration, the resulting maps represent common-template source estimates rather than participant-specific\nanatomical localizations.\n3.5\n\nClustering branch patterns across participants\n\nFor the participant–branch analysis, we computed the spatial and temporal patterns described in Section 2.3 for each\nof the K = 25 branches and all 27 participants, using the Cable Spool Fort recordings from the first session. Before\ncomputing these patterns, we subtracted the mean from each learned temporal kernel to remove its DC component.\nThe sensor-space spatial pattern was projected onto the common cortical source space; we retained the absolute source\namplitudes and normalized each resulting map to unit L2 norm. The temporal representation was the 15-sample pattern\nof Eq. (7). Its FFT magnitude was used for visualisation, whereas its log-magnitude spectrum was used to compare\nbranches. This produced 27 × 25 = 675 participant–branch items, each represented by a cortical source-magnitude\nmap and a temporal spectrum.\nsrc\nFor items i and j, we computed the Pearson correlation rij\nbetween their cortical source-magnitude maps and the\ntemp\nPearson correlation rij between their log-magnitude spectra. We combined the two views through the distance\n(\n)\ntemp\nsrc\n+1\nrij\n+ 1 rij\ndij = 1 − min\n,\n.\n(17)\n2\n2\n\nThis weakest-view combination makes two branches close only when both their cortical distributions and their temporal\nfrequency profiles are similar.\nBefore clustering, we screened the sensor-space spatial patterns for excessive local roughness. Roughness was defined\nas the mean squared difference between each sensor value and the values at its six nearest sensors, divided by the\nvariance of the pattern across sensors. Patterns with roughness greater than 1 were excluded from clustering.\nWe applied complete-linkage agglomerative clustering to all remaining participant–branch items and cut the hierarchy\nat a minimum raw Pearson correlation of τ = 0.25 in both views, corresponding to a distance threshold of (1 − τ )/2 =\n0.375 in Eq. (17). Taking the minimum across views makes the pairwise criterion strict: two branches can be close only\nif both their source maps and temporal spectra are similar. Complete linkage then makes the cluster-level criterion strict\nby enforcing this bound for the least similar pair in a cluster. Because a single low-similarity pair can therefore block\na merge, we used τ = 0.25 as a deliberately permissive worst-case bound rather than as the expected within-cluster\nsimilarity. Consequently, every pair within a resulting cluster had both raw correlations of at least 0.25.\nEach cluster was represented by its medoid: the branch with the highest mean weakest-view similarity to the other\nmembers of that cluster.\n3.6\n\nPaired MEG occlusion analysis of stimulus-feature use\n\nTo determine which stimulus-linked neural information supports the trained decoder’s retrieval decisions, we tested\na battery of candidate features spanning broad acoustic state, rapid spectral change, phonetic identity, lexical timing,\ncontextual predictability, and experimentally inserted disruptions of the narrative. The analysis contrasts matched MEG\nsubstitutions associated with the presence and absence of each feature. We therefore first define the candidate features\nand their control states before describing the substitution procedure.\nStimulus features. We evaluated 19 acoustic, phonetic, and linguistic features on the same 100 Hz audio timeline used\nto construct the MEG–audio pairs. Four binary features described complete silent periods, silence onsets corresponding\nto speech cessation, silence offsets corresponding to speech resumption, and the union of the two boundary masks.\nSeven further features marked vowels, stops, fricatives, sibilants, nasals, liquids/glides, and schwa using Montreal\nForced Aligner TextGrid annotations. These phoneme groups were not mutually exclusive: sibilants were also included\namong fricatives, and schwa among vowels.\nA separate word-onset feature marked the beginning of every spoken word, including words occurring within continuous\nspeech rather than only after silence. Two features captured the dataset’s controlled departures from natural narrative\nspeech. Pseudowords were non-words inserted within otherwise coherent sentences, providing spoken sequences\nwithout an associated lexical meaning. Random word lists consisted of words taken from the preceding approximately\nfive minutes of the story and presented in random order. This preserved individual words while removing normal\nsentence and narrative structure. The corresponding masks covered the full spoken duration of each pseudoword or\nword-list segment.\n9\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\nThe continuous linguistic features were word surprisal, predictive entropy, and word frequency. Surprisal was computed\nwith GPT-2 as the summed negative log2 probability of the subword tokens forming each word. Predictive entropy was\nthe entropy of the model’s next-token distribution immediately before the word, and frequency was measured on the\nZipf scale. The remaining continuous features described basic acoustic structure. Loudness was an energy-based proxy\ncomputed as the log mean-square waveform energy in non-overlapping 10 ms frames. Acoustic-onset strength was a\nspectral-flux envelope computed from a centred log-power mel spectrogram using a 128 ms analysis window and a\n10 ms hop, and therefore measured rapid changes in the stimulus spectrum.\nFeature states and controls. For the binary features, the annotated intervals constituted the feature-present state. The\ncontinuous features were converted into binary contrasts. For surprisal, predictive entropy, loudness, and acoustic-onset\nstrength, the upper and lower quartiles defined the feature-present and feature-absent states, respectively. For word\nfrequency, the direction was reversed so that rare and frequent words formed the feature-present and feature-absent\nstates. Quartile thresholds for the word-level features were estimated from ordinary narrative words in the five non-test\npieces of Black Willow; pseudowords and words presented in the random word-list segments were excluded from\nthreshold estimation.\nThe feature-absent state was chosen to provide a meaningful control rather than a generic complement whenever\npossible. For each phoneme class, the control comprised other labelled phonemes. For pseudoword and random\nword-list insertions, it comprised unmanipulated narrative words. For the remaining binary features, it comprised time\noutside the marked feature intervals. Feature-present masks were expanded by 20 ms on either side to accommodate\nsmall timing errors. When a predefined control mask was available, it was expanded likewise and any resulting overlap\nwith the feature-present mask was removed. Otherwise, the feature-absent donor pool was defined as all time outside\nthe expanded feature-present mask.\nPaired MEG substitution. The 1005 test queries were the 3 s windows from the final seven pieces of Black Willow.\nReal-MEG donor intervals were drawn from the five non-test pieces of the same story. For every participant and session,\nwe selected test windows that contained the feature but were not completely covered by it. All feature-positive intervals\nwithin an eligible query were replaced simultaneously in two conditions:\nX f →0 : replacement by MEG from a feature-absent interval,\nX\n\nf →f\n\n: replacement by MEG from a feature-present interval.\n\n(18)\n(19)\n\nThe feature-present substitution served as a matched control for the replacement procedure itself. Comparing X f →0\ndirectly with the unmodified query would conflate the effect of changing the feature state with generic consequences of\nreplacing the original MEG, including loss of query-specific activity, donor mismatch, and blending at the replacement\nboundaries. In contrast, both substitution arms replaced the same intervals with real donor MEG under the same\nmatching and tapering procedure, while differing in whether the donor interval was associated with the feature. The\nunmodified-query ranks were retained as a descriptive baseline for quantifying the total retrieval damage produced by\neach substitution arm, but were not used as the primary feature-specific contrast.\nDonor intervals had exactly the same duration as the replaced interval and came from the same participant and session as\nthe target query. Within each matched pair, the feature-present and feature-absent donors came from the same audio file;\nthe absent interval was selected as the nearest available match to a randomly sampled present interval. All 208 channels\nwere copied together, and a 20 ms raised-cosine taper blended each replacement into the original signal. Because\nmultiple valid donor intervals were available, an effect estimated from a single donor pair could depend substantially\non that particular random selection. We therefore evaluated each eligible query using five matched donor pairs and\naveraged over these repetitions in the rank contrast below, reducing variability attributable to donor selection. These\nrepetitions were used for averaging and were not treated as independent observations. The fixed 150 ms MEG–audio\nlatency used during training and testing was retained when donor intervals were extracted.\nImportantly, windows without the feature, windows fully covered by the feature, and windows for which a complete set\nof matched donor pairs was unavailable were excluded only from the feature-specific query average. They remained in\nthe unchanged 1005-candidate retrieval bank for every rank calculation.\nf →0\nf →f\nRank contrast. For eligible query i and donor pair j ∈ {1, . . . , 5}, let rsqij\nand rsqij\ndenote the rank of the correct\naudio candidate for participant s and session q. Rank was defined as one plus the number of candidates with a cosine\nsimilarity strictly greater than that of the correct candidate. The subject–session effect for feature f was\n5\n\nEsqf =\n\n\u0011\n1 X X \u0010 f →0\nf →f\nrsqij − rsqij\n,\n5|If |\nj=1\ni∈If\n\n10\n\n(20)\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\nwhere If is the set of eligible test queries. Positive values indicate that replacing the marked intervals with feature-absent\nMEG worsened retrieval more than the matched feature-present control. For participants with two sessions, the two\nsession effects were averaged so that every participant contributed one value,\nQs\n\n1 X\nDsf =\nEsqf .\nQs q=1\nStatistical inference.\n\n(21)\n\nFor each feature, the observed statistic was the studentized participant mean,\nTf =\n\nDf\n√ ,\nSD(D1f , . . . , DSf )/ S\n\nS = 27.\n\n(22)\n\nWe tested the directional alternative E[Dsf ] > 0 using Nperm = 100,000 participant sign-flip permutations. In each\npermutation, one random sign was applied to the complete 19-feature vector of each participant, thereby preserving\ndependencies among features within participants. Familywise error was controlled with a one-sided single-step max-T\n(b)\n(b)\ncorrection. If Tf is the permuted statistic and M (b) = maxg Tg , the corrected value was\n\u0003\nPNperm \u0002 (b)\n1 + b=1\n1 M ≥ Tf\nFWER\npf\n=\n.\n(23)\nNperm + 1\nFeature-present control and significance criterion. A difference between the two substitution conditions is interpretable only if the feature-present substitution preserves meaningful retrieval. If replacing the marked intervals with\nfeature-present MEG already reduces retrieval to random ranking, the contrast with the feature-absent substitution may\nreflect different degrees of generic disruption rather than the decoder’s use of the tested feature. We therefore used the\nf → f condition as a validity check for each feature.\nAfter averaging donor pairs and query windows within each participant–session and then sessions within each participant,\nwe computed the mean f → f rank across the 27 participants. Under random ordering of 1005 candidates, the correct\ntarget is equally likely to occupy any rank from 1 to 1005, so the expected mean rank is\n1 + 1005\n= 503.\n(24)\n2\nThe feature-present control was considered valid when its mean rank was below 503. This check was performed once\nper feature and was not used to exclude individual query windows.\nE[rrandom ] =\n\nTherefore, a feature was marked as showing a significant positive occlusion effect only when all three criteria were\nmet: the mean participant-level contrast Df was positive, the one-sided participant sign-flip max-T test yielded\npFWER\n< 0.05, and the mean rank in the f → f condition was below 503.\nf\n3.7\n\nDataset and preprocessing\n\nWe use the MEG-MASC dataset [7], comprising audio and MEG signals from 49 approximately one-hour participant–\nsession recordings obtained from 27 English-speaking participants. Twenty-two participants contributed two sessions\nand five contributed one. In each available session, participants listened to four fictional stories from the MASC corpus.\nThe study was approved by the institutional review board ethics committee of New York University Abu Dhabi [7].\nAudio was resampled to 16 kHz and divided into 3 s windows with a 1 s stride. We discarded windows whose peak\nabsolute waveform amplitude was below 10−4 . A window was retained only if at least 50% of the duration of one or\nmore annotated words fell within it. The target representation for each retained window was obtained with the wav2vec\n2.0 Base model [2] by averaging its final four hidden layers at each model time step.\nOcular and cardiac ICA components were removed from the MEG recordings before further preprocessing. The data\nwere resampled from 1000 to 100 Hz. Separately for each participant–session–story recording, we subtracted the\nper-channel mean over the 0.5 s immediately preceding the first stimulus onset, robust-scaled each channel using its\nmedian and interquartile range, standardized it to zero mean and unit variance, and clipped the resulting values to ±20\nstandard deviations.\nThe entire stories LW1, Cable Spool Fort, and Easy Money, together with the first five pieces of Black Willow, formed a\n2698-segment development set. Within this set, the fifth piece of Black Willow was held out for validation and excluded\nfrom neural-network parameter fitting. The last seven pieces of Black Willow formed the test set of 1005 candidate\n11\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\nsegments. For Black Willow, the scaling parameters were fitted only on samples preceding the first test-piece onset to\navoid test leakage. Each 3 s audio segment was paired with the 3 s MEG segment beginning 150 ms later to account for\nauditory-response latency, similarly to [1]. In contrast to [1], we did not align test segments to word onsets, resulting in\na less structured and therefore more challenging retrieval setting.\n3.8\n\nTraining and testing\n\nModels were trained with the AdamW optimizer [48], using a learning rate of 3 × 10−4 for the network parameters and\n10−3 for the learned temperature, zero weight decay, a batch size of 100, and at most 50 epochs. Early stopping used a\npatience of seven epochs and checkpoint selection was based on the lowest validation loss. Unless stated otherwise, the\nreported main checkpoint, architectural sweeps, ablations, and interpretation analyses use seed 42. All experiments used\nthe L = 24 spherical-harmonic setting defined in Section 3.1, corresponding to degrees 0, . . . , 23 and 576 real basis\nfunctions per virtual channel. The training objective was a one-directional MEG-to-audio contrastive cross-entropy loss.\nMEG-derived embeddings were compared with the unique audio targets represented in the current minibatch. Within\neach minibatch, when multiple MEG-derived embeddings corresponded to the same audio-segment target, they were\nall assigned to the same target embedding. The resulting similarity matrix therefore had one row per MEG-derived\nembedding and U columns, where U is the number of unique audio targets in the minibatch. Using this matrix instead\nof a square batch-by-batch similarity matrix prevents duplicate instances of the same positive audio target from being\ntreated as negatives in the cross-entropy loss. Similarities were computed after L2 normalization over the feature–time\ndimensions and divided by a learned temperature parameter. Training was performed on a cluster node with a single\nNVIDIA Tesla V100 32 GB GPU and 8 cores of Intel Xeon Gold 6152 CPU.\nTo estimate the effect of a window size, we also evaluated paired MEG and audio segments of W ∈ {1.5, 2.25, 3, 4, 5} s.\nWe retained the start positions of the original 3 s segments rather than constructing a new set of segments at each\nduration. For an anchor at time t, the audio target was regenerated directly from the continuous recording over [t, t + W ],\nand the corresponding MEG input covered [t + 150 ms, t + W + 150 ms]. Wav2vec 2.0 audio embeddings were then\nrecomputed separately for each window size.\nAs longer window sizes usually result in fewer windows, to keep the training-set size and retrieval-database size\nconstant across durations, we used a common set of window starts containing only start positions for which a complete\n5 s segment remained within the audio. This yielded 991 test candidates at every duration. Separate models with\nK = 25 branches and B ∈ {0, 2, 5} convolutional blocks were trained for each duration, with all other training and\ncheckpoint-selection procedures kept unchanged. The 3 s ablation condition was regenerated using this same pipeline\nand common anchor set rather than copied from the main 1005-candidate experiment.\nTo examine the temporal scale required by the interpretable front end, we trained otherwise identical two-block,\nK = 25 models with temporal-filter kernel sizes of 1, 5, 9, 15, 19, 25, 29, and 49 samples, corresponding to supports\nof 10–490 ms at the 100 Hz MEG sampling rate. All conditions used seed 42 and the same data split, optimizer,\nearly-stopping rule, and validation-based checkpoint selection. The one-sample condition contains no temporal context\nand acts only as a learnable scaling of each branch.\n\n4\n\nResults\n\n4.1\n\nDecoding accuracy\n\nWe assess decoding efficacy on the held-out pieces 5–11 of The Black Willow. We first evaluated how retrieval\nperformance depends on two architectural capacity parameters: the number of interpretable branches K and the number\nof convolutional blocks following the network’s front end. For each configuration, we report test Top-1 and Top-10\naccuracy at the checkpoint with the lowest validation loss, so the sweep reflects validation-based model selection rather\nthan test-set checkpoint tuning (Fig. 3).\nBoth metrics show a sharp improvement when K increases from very small values to the intermediate regime. With\nonly one or a few branches, the model is strongly bottlenecked: the branch space is too small to preserve the information\nneeded to distinguish the correct audio segment from the candidate set. Performance then rises rapidly and reaches a\nbroad high-accuracy plateau around K = 10–25. This is the main trend of the sweep: tens of branches are sufficient to\nrecover most of the retrieval performance, while increasing K toward hundreds does not produce systematic gains.\nThe behavior at large K is also informative. If the branch dimension were simply acting as generic model capacity, one\nwould expect performance to keep improving, or at least remain monotonic, as K grows. Instead, several curves flatten\nand then mildly decline at larger K. This suggests that the useful task-related subspace is compact, and that adding\n12\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\nTop-1 accuracy\n\n0.4\n\n0.3\n\n0.2\n\n0.1\n\n0.6\n0.5\n0.4\nTemporal blocks\n\n5 Blocks\n\n250\n\n4 Blocks\n\n2 Blocks\n\n200\n\n3 Blocks\n\n1 Block\n\n150\n\n50\n\n25\n\n10\n\n5\n\n1\n\n0.2\n\n0 Blocks\n\n100\n\n0.3\n\n75\n\nTop-10 accuracy\n\n0.7\n\nK (unmix channels)\n\nFigure 3: Retrieval accuracy as a function of the number of interpretable branches K and the number of convolutional\nblocks in the decoder. For each configuration, Top-1 and Top-10 test accuracy are reported at the checkpoint with the\nlowest validation loss. Accuracy increases sharply from very small K to approximately K = 10–25, then enters a\nbroad plateau; larger K does not produce systematic gains and can mildly degrade performance. Across decoder depths,\nthe 0-block model is consistently weaker, while models with 2–5 convolutional blocks form a similar high-performing\nregime. The main 2-conv, K = 25 configuration lies on this compact high-accuracy plateau.\n\nmany extra branch channels mostly increases redundant or harder-to-regularize degrees of freedom rather than adding\nnew decodable information.\nConvolutional depth modulates this pattern but does not change the overall conclusion. The model without convolutional\nblocks is consistently weaker once K enters the useful range, showing that the compact branch representation still\nneeds downstream temporal/contextual processing. Adding convolutional blocks substantially improves retrieval, but\nthe gains saturate: models with two or more blocks occupy a similar high-performing regime over the plateau. Deeper\nvariants can match or slightly exceed the two-block model at some values of K, but they do not produce a qualitatively\ndifferent dependence on branch dimensionality.\n4.2\n\nModified attention scores\n\nFigure 4 compares the mean softmax-normalized attention weights across the J = 270 virtual channels,\nJ","numLines":600,"startLine":400,"totalLines":3131}}}
