{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aba6c11facc4308c","type":"tool_result","content":"1000\t\n1001\tc̄m =\n1002\t\n1003\t100 X\n1004\te\n1005\tcjm ,\n1006\tJ j=1\n1007\t\n1008\t(25)\n1009\t\n1010\tfor architectures with varying numbers of non-linear convolutional blocks (B) and branch counts (K = 5, 10, 25).\n1011\tAcross the grid, increasing decoder depth and branch count is generally accompanied by progressively more spatially\n1012\tstructured and concentrated attention. This trend is reflected in the reduction of Neff , the effective number of sensors\n1013\t13\n1014\t\n1015\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n1016\t\n1017\t0\n1018\t\n1019\t1\n1020\t\n1021\t2\n1022\t\n1023\t3\n1024\t\n1025\t4\n1026\t\n1027\t5\n1028\t\n1029\tNumber of branches (K)\n1030\t\n1031\t1.0\n1032\tNeff = 178\n1033\t\n1034\tNeff = 179\n1035\t\n1036\tNeff = 169\n1037\t\n1038\tNeff = 175\n1039\t\n1040\tNeff = 169\n1041\t\n1042\tNeff = 176\n1043\t\n1044\t0.8\n1045\t\n1046\t10\n1047\t0.6\n1048\t\n1049\tNeff = 175\n1050\t\n1051\tNeff = 169\n1052\t\n1053\tNeff = 163\n1054\t\n1055\tNeff = 159\n1056\t\n1057\tNeff = 162\n1058\t\n1059\tNeff = 150\n1060\t\n1061\t0.4\n1062\t\n1063\tMean attention weight (%)\n1064\t\n1065\t1.2\n1066\t\n1067\t5\n1068\t\n1069\t0.2\n1070\t\n1071\t25\n1072\t0.0\n1073\tNeff = 169\n1074\t\n1075\tNeff = 164\n1076\t\n1077\tNeff = 159\n1078\t\n1079\tNeff = 164\n1080\t\n1081\tNeff = 152\n1082\t\n1083\tNeff = 152\n1084\t\n1085\tNumber of temporal blocks\n1086\tFigure 4: 3D spherical-harmonic attention learned by architectures\n1087\tP 2 −1 with varying numbers of non-linear convolutional\n1088\tblocks (B) and branch\n1089\tP counts (K = 5, 10, 25). Neff = ( m pm ) is the inverse-Simpson effective number of sensors,\n1090\twhere pm = c̄m / m′ c̄m′ ; smaller values indicate that attention is concentrated on fewer sensors. For visualization\n1091\tmaximal value was capped to 99-th percentile, but all Neff are computed with full attention weights without clipping.\n1092\t\n1093\tcarrying the attention mass. Because every map is normalized to the same total attention mass, these differences\n1094\trepresent a redistribution of attention across sensors rather than a change in its overall magnitude.\n1095\tDespite this evolution, the maps consistently highlight sensors overlying primary auditory cortical areas as well as more\n1096\tanterior regions. In the most complex configuration, the attention appears more concentrated over the left hemisphere\n1097\tand more broadly distributed over the right. These localizations remain approximate because the displayed maps\n1098\tdescribe only the first shared spatial-attention stage. Complete participant-specific spatial filtering is defined by the rows\n1099\tof W(s) in Eq. (10), obtained from the product of the three matrices in the spatial-filtering block shown in Figure 2.\n1100\tThe rows of W(s) permit a principled interpretation according to the methodology outlined in Section 3.4. These results\n1101\tare presented next.\n1102\t4.3\n1103\t\n1104\tCross-participant spatial structure\n1105\t\n1106\tAnalysis of the spatial topographies aggregated over 27 subjects allows us to observe a more detailed structure of the\n1107\tneuronal assemblies underlying the decision rule discovered by the network as compared to the spatial attention weights.\n1108\tTo probe the cross-subject structure of the learned representations we aggregated the spatial filters from all interpretable\n1109\tbranches across all 27 subjects: for each of the 25 branches in the subject-specific interpretable block, we collected the\n1110\tcorresponding 208 × 1 spatial filter for each participant, yielding 675 spatial filters in total. For this analysis, spatial\n1111\tpatterns were computed from the MEG recording obtained during the first session of Cable Spool Fort. We excluded\n1112\t10 s from each end of the recording and used the zero-mean temporal kernels defined in Eq. (16). Each spatial filter\n1113\tand spatial pattern was normalized to unit L2 norm before SVD. We then computed spatial patterns following [8] and\n1114\tperformed SVD on both the filter and pattern matrices, extracting the leading ten singular vectors from each. The\n1115\tdominant spatial patterns in Figure 5 exhibit interpretable structure including bilateral temporal activity consistent\n1116\twith auditory cortex engagement, as well as frontal and central patterns potentially reflecting motor preparation or\n1117\tattention-related processes. While most patterns are dipolar, some exhibit a clear quadrupolar structure centered over\n1118\tthe primary auditory cortices reflecting complex spatial-temporal dynamics of the pivotal neuronal populations.\n1119\t14\n1120\t\n1121\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n1122\t\n1123\t1\n1124\t\n1125\t2\n1126\t\n1127\t3\n1128\t\n1129\t4\n1130\t\n1131\t5\n1132\t\n1133\t6\n1134\t\n1135\t7\n1136\t\n1137\t8\n1138\t\n1139\t9\n1140\t\n1141\t10\n1142\t\n1143\tFilters\n1144\t\n1145\tTopographies\n1146\t\n1147\tFilters\n1148\t\n1149\tTopographies\n1150\t\n1151\t(a) First ten singular vectors calculated separately for all L2 -normed spatial filters and topographies.\n1152\t\n1153\tAxial\n1154\t50\n1155\t\n1156\t25\n1157\t50\n1158\t\n1159\t50\n1160\t\n1161\tZ (mm)\n1162\t\n1163\tZ (mm)\n1164\t\n1165\t0\n1166\t\n1167\tSagittal\n1168\t\n1169\t100\n1170\t\n1171\t50\n1172\t\n1173\t25\n1174\t\n1175\tY (mm)\n1176\t\n1177\tCoronal\n1178\t\n1179\t100\n1180\t\n1181\t75\n1182\t\n1183\t0\n1184\t50\n1185\t\n1186\t0\n1187\t50\n1188\t\n1189\t75\n1190\t100\n1191\t\n1192\t100\n1193\t\n1194\t100\n1195\t50\n1196\t\n1197\t0\n1198\t\n1199\tX (mm)\n1200\t\n1201\t50\n1202\t\n1203\t50\n1204\t\n1205\t0\n1206\t\n1207\tX (mm)\n1208\t\n1209\t50\n1210\t\n1211\t100\n1212\t\n1213\t50\n1214\t\n1215\t0\n1216\t\n1217\tY (mm)\n1218\t\n1219\t50\n1220\t\n1221\t(b) Dominant dipoles derived from the cross-subject spatial patterns of Figure 5a using the RAP-MUSIC algorithm [45] (subspace\n1222\tcorrelation threshold 0.8). Dipoles cluster in bilateral auditory cortices, medial temporal lobe and frontal lobe on the fsaverage\n1223\tanatomy.\n1224\t\n1225\tFigure 5: Leading ten singular vectors of the across-subject spatial filter and spatial pattern matrices aggregated from\n1226\tthe interpretable branches of all 27 subjects, and the equivalent current dipoles fitted to the subspace spanned by these\n1227\ttopographies. We can clearly observe the involvement of not only the primary auditory cortices but also frontal and\n1228\tmedial temporal lobe structures.\n1229\t\n1230\tUnlike the spatial attention weights in Figure 4 the spatial topography patterns from Figure 5 result from the principled\n1231\tapproach to jointly treat the spatial and temporal processing happening in each branch of the network’s front-end [4].\n1232\tThis approach recognizes the role of both the spatial and the temporal filters in not only tuning to the target sources but\n1233\t15\n1234\t\n1235\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n1236\t\n1237\talso de-tuning from the interference. It also permits the use of inverse modeling approaches to localize pivotal neuronal\n1238\tsources.\n1239\tTo roughly localize the sources underlying these dominant spatial patterns, we applied a variant of RAP–MUSIC [45]\n1240\tjointly to the subspace spanned by the top ten singular vectors of the spatial-pattern matrix. At each recursion,\n1241\twe selected the cortical location with the highest subspace correlation and projected the full local three-orientation\n1242\tlead-field subspace from both the spatial-pattern subspace and the remaining candidate lead fields, as described in\n1243\tAppendix A. Using a subspace-correlation threshold of 0.8, the procedure identified the candidate source locations\n1244\tshown on the fsaverage anatomy in Figure 5b. Their distribution across bilateral auditory cortices supports the\n1245\tfunctional plausibility of the extracted subspace and of the cross-participant spatial structure learned by the network.\n1246\t4.4\n1247\t\n1248\tClustered spatial and temporal patterns\n1249\t\n1250\tFigure 6 shows the medoids of the twelve largest (out of a total of 49) spatial–spectral clusters obtained as described in\n1251\tSection 3.5. Each comprises the 150 ms temporal pattern computed with the zero-mean kernel, its magnitude spectrum,\n1252\tand the spatial topography with the corresponding common-template MNE source-magnitude estimate in the left and\n1253\tright hemispheres.\n1254\tWithin-cluster agreement is considerably higher than the partition threshold, which is defined by the least similar pair in\n1255\teach cluster. Across the twelve, median within-cluster correlations range from 0.51 to 0.67 for the cortical source maps\n1256\tand from 0.63 to 0.72 for the temporal spectra. Pooling all within-cluster pairs, 93.7% correlate at 0.40 or above in\n1257\tboth views and 71.1% at 0.50 or above. Seven of the 675 patterns failed the spatial-roughness screen and were excluded\n1258\tbefore clustering.\n1259\tThe retained clusters fall into three groups whose locations map onto the standard anatomy of speech perception.\n1260\tThe largest is located bilaterally along the superior temporal gyrus and superior temporal plane, where continuous\n1261\ttracking of the speech envelope has been localized with both invasive and non-invasive recordings [49–53] and where\n1262\tthe acoustic-edge response has been reported [54]. This interpretation is also consistent with the paired occlusion\n1263\tanalysis in Section 4.5, where replacing silent, loud, vowel and acoustic-onset intervals with feature-absent donor MEG\n1264\tdegrades retrieval most.\n1265\tA second group extends posteriorly and superiorly toward the supramarginal region and the bank of the central sulcus.\n1266\tThese are dorsal-stream sites in the dual-stream account [55], which assigns phonological and sensorimotor functions\n1267\tto posterior temporal and inferior parietal cortex. Their presence is consistent with the smaller but reliable effects for\n1268\tfricatives, stops and sibilants, and with reports that passive speech perception recruits articulatory representations [16,\n1269\t18, 19].\n1270\tA third and spatially tighter group appears in the left frontal operculum and adjacent inferior frontal cortex, together with\n1271\tmore anterior middle and inferior temporal foci. Functionally the location aligns with the accounts in which temporallobe perceptual predictions are reconciled in inferior frontal cortex [56], and the temporal foci fit auditory–conceptual\n1272\tintegration [57].\n1273\tIntriguingly, we can notice a sustained hemispheric difference in the spectra of the retained clusters. Branches whose\n1274\tsource estimates lateralize to the left hemisphere show two components in their temporal patterns: a dominant slow\n1275\tpeak below 10 Hz and a second, weaker component at 13.3 Hz corresponding to the third effective FFT bin (filter with\n1276\t15 taps, 100 Hz sampling rate). Branches lateralizing to the right are dominantly low frequency single-peaked, with\n1277\tpower concentrated around 6.6 Hz. The slow component is common to both hemispheres and is consistent with an\n1278\tevoked response. The faster component appears predominantly on the left. This pattern is reminiscent of the asymmetric\n1279\tsampling in time account of auditory lateralization. Poeppel [58] proposed that the two auditory cortices integrate\n1280\tover different windows, with the left biased toward short windows suited to rapid acoustic transitions and the right\n1281\ttoward longer windows suited to slower envelope and prosodic structure. Boemio et al. [59] found empirical support for\n1282\tthis division, reporting that more slowly modulated signals preferentially drive the right hemisphere in higher-order\n1283\tsuperior temporal cortex. The later oscillatory formulation of the same idea holds that these integration windows are set\n1284\tby intrinsic cortical rhythms, with delta and theta dominant on the right and faster rhythms available on the left [60].\n1285\tTherefore our observations are in line with this prediction that branch spectra that carry an additional faster component\n1286\twould be dominantly focused on the left hemisphere sources.\n1287\tThe faster component sits near the alpha–beta boundary. Rhythms in this range have been reported during naturalistic\n1288\tspeech comprehension and associated with predictive and top-down contributions rather than with acoustic tracking [61].\n1289\tVisual analysis of our branch-wise decomposition is consistent with that hemispheric division of labor: the slow\n1290\tcomponent, present in both hemispheres, would track the speech envelope, while the faster left-lateralized component\n1291\twould reflect the additional processing that supports lexical and contextual structure.\n1292\t16\n1293\t\n1294\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n1295\t\n1296\tTwo limits bound the observation. The temporal filters are 15 samples long at 100 Hz, which gives a frequency\n1297\tresolution of roughly 6.7 Hz. What matters is the relative magnitude of these ripples. In the future we are planning to\n1298\tfurther scrutinize this result to move beyond mere observation and see the reproducibility of this pattern across the\n1299\tparticipants. Our filter-length experiment, see Figure 13, found the 490 ms model to be numerically best, although gains\n1300\tbeyond 150 ms were non-monotonic. Longer filters also provide finer frequency resolution, making it useful to test\n1301\twhether the observed spectral lateralization persists when estimated with longer kernels.\n1302\tCluster 2\n1303\t\n1304\tCluster 3\n1305\t\n1306\tCluster 4\n1307\t\n1308\tCluster 5\n1309\t\n1310\tCluster 6\n1311\t\n1312\tTemporal pattern\n1313\t\n1314\t0.1\n1315\t\n1316\tSpectrum\n1317\t\n1318\tTopography\n1319\t\n1320\tCluster 1\n