w\n1724\ten\n1725\tce a\n1726\tSil edge\n1727\ten\n1728\tce s\n1729\ton\n1730\ts\n1731\tRa et\n1732\tre\n1733\two\n1734\tr\n1735\tWo d\n1736\trd\n1737\tlist\n1738\t\n1739\t50\n1740\t\n1741\tGroup effect (+)\n1742\t\n1743\tGroup effect ( )\n1744\t\n1745\tSubject\n1746\t\n1747\tSign-flip null\n1748\t\n1749\tpFWER < 0.05\n1750\t\n1751\tFigure 7: Paired MEG occlusion effects for 19 stimulus features. For each participant, the plotted effect is the retrievalrank difference between feature-absent replacement (“removal”) and matched feature-present replacement (“control”),\n1752\tafter averaging donor realisations, eligible queries, and multiple sessions. Positive values indicate that preserving\n1753\tfeature-associated MEG information retained a better rank. Grey points show participant effects and diamonds show\n1754\tgroup means. Green violins show the feature-wise sign-flip null distributions in rank-difference units; stars mark\n1755\tone-sided single-step max-T familywise-error-corrected p < 0.05. Every rank was computed against the complete,\n1756\tunchanged bank of 1005 candidates. Because the masks differed in duration and in their sets of eligible queries, effect\n1757\tmagnitudes should not be read as a calibrated ranking of feature-encoding strength across features.\n1758\t\n1759\t4.5\n1760\t\n1761\tPaired occlusion identifies stimulus-associated MEG information used for retrieval\n1762\t\n1763\tThe paired rank contrast ∆rf = rf →0 − rf →f was positive after familywise-error correction for 15 of the 19 tested\n1764\tfeatures (Figure 7). Thus, for these features, real MEG from feature-present intervals preserved the correct candidate rank\n1765\tbetter than equally sized replacements from feature-absent intervals. None of the feature-present controls approached\n1766\trandom ranking: their participant-balanced mean ranks ranged from 23.2 to 153.5, compared with an expected rank of\n1767\t503 under random ordering. Because the substitution replaces more MEG for long contiguous events such as silence\n1768\tthan for brief ones such as word onsets, the ordering of effect magnitudes across features is confounded by the duration\n1769\tof the replaced intervals, so the ranking should be read as identifying which features the decoder uses rather than how\n1770\tmuch each contributes.\n1771\tThe largest observed contrasts were obtained for silence (∆r = 75.62), high loudness (60.77), vowels (38.70), and\n1772\tstrong acoustic onset (36.19); all had pFWER < 10−4 , and the effect was positive in all 27 participants. These results\n1773\tshow that the decoder uses MEG distinctions associated with broad speech state, sound intensity, rapid acoustic change,\n1774\tand phonetic content.\n1775\tFurther corrected positive effects were observed for fricatives (∆r = 12.80), stops (9.27), sibilants (7.94), and high\n1776\tsurprisal (7.34). Smaller effects remained significant for pseudowords (1.71, pFWER = 0.022), silence offsets (1.60),\n1777\tliquids/glides (1.21), nasals (1.18), word onsets (1.02), schwa (0.96), and the combined silence-edge mask (0.91);\n1778\texcept for pseudowords, these effects had pFWER < 10−4 .\n1779\tWe found no corrected positive evidence for high predictive entropy (∆r = 1.40, pFWER = 0.069), silence onsets\n1780\t(0.05, pFWER = 0.316), rare words (−2.98), or random word lists (−17.77).\n1781\t18\n1782\t\n1783\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n1784\t\n1785\tThe observed negativity of the random word-list effect is interesting and given our paired occlusion design we suggest\n1786\tthe following interpretation. Since the feature-absent donor for word lists corresponds to unmanipulated narrative\n1787\tspeech, narrative MEG substituted into a word-list interval supports retrieval better than the MEG recorded during the\n1788\tword-list presentation. Cortical activity recorded during randomly ordered words therefore carries less recoverable\n1789\tinformation about the audio than the activity recorded during coherent narrative, which converges with reports of\n1790\treduced neural tracking of language features when sentence and narrative structure are removed [62].\n1791\tWe would expect the opposite result as the semantic incongruence is known to supply an additional evoked response\n1792\tavailable to the decoder, as indexed by the N400 [63, 64]. However a closer look allows us to resolve this potential\n1793\tcontradiction. Note that the data set contains two different insertions: pseudowords and the words from the unrelated\n1794\twords list. A pseudoword violates an expectation established by the surrounding sentence, whereas a random word\n1795\tlist removes the context that would generate such expectations in the first place. The observed contrast between the\n1796\tsmall (yet significant) positive pseudoword effect and the negative word-list effect follows that distinction, and may\n1797\tsuggest that what the decoder loses during word lists is not a violation response but the predictive structure that supports\n1798\ttracking in coherent speech. The feature-use pattern was remarkably stable across model initializations. All 15 features\n1799\twith a corrected positive effect in the seed-42 model remained positive and met the same criterion in all six trained\n1800\tmodels, while the random-word-list contrast remained negative in all six (Appendix B).\n1801\t4.6\n1802\t\n1803\tEffect of segment duration on retrieval\n1804\t\n1805\tRetrieval accuracy increased monotonically with paired MEG–audio segment duration for all three decoder depths\n1806\t(Figure 8). For the main two-block model, Top-1 accuracy increased from 14.37% at 1.5 s to 28.03% at 2.25 s, 39.94%\n1807\tat 3 s, 52.66% at 4 s, and 62.20% at 5 s. Top-10 accuracy followed the same progression, increasing from 41.00% to\n1808\t59.52%, 70.84%, 80.31%, and 86.33%, respectively.\n1809\tThe same duration dependence was present with zero and five convolutional blocks. The five-block model closely\n1810\tfollowed the two-block model, reaching 62.38% Top-1 and 86.27% Top-10 accuracy at 5 s. Removing the convolutional\n1811\tblocks reduced performance at every duration, although the increase with segment length remained monotonic. The\n1812\tzero-block model reached 58.24% Top-1 and 83.37% Top-10 accuracy at 5 s. Thus, the benefit of longer segments was\n1813\trobust to decoder depth, while the downstream convolutional module provided an additional but comparatively modest\n1814\timprovement.\n1815\tThe complete rank curves showed that the improvement was not restricted to Top-1 or Top-10 accuracy (Figure 9).\n1816\tFor the main two-block model, we estimated Top-n accuracy at different cutoffs from Top-1 to Top-50. The ordering\n1817\tby segment duration was preserved at every retrieval cutoff. At n = 50, accuracy increased from 65.88% for 1.5 s\n1818\tsegments to 80.26%, 87.32%, 92.47%, and 95.37% for 2.25, 3, 4, and 5 s segments, respectively. Longer paired\n1819\tsegments therefore improved both exact retrieval and the broader ranking of the correct audio candidate.\n1820\tBecause the MEG input and the audio target were lengthened together, this experiment measures the effect of paired\n1821\tsegment duration rather than isolating neural context integration alone. The gains may reflect both the additional\n1822\tneural information available in a longer MEG segment and the greater amount or distinctiveness of information in the\n1823\tcorresponding audio target.\n1824\t4.7\n1825\t\n1826\tFeature-space compression\n1827\t\n1828\tWe first tested whether the full wav2vec feature dimension is necessary for retrieval, or whether the MEG-decodable\n1829\tpart of the target representation lies in a much smaller feature subspace. In all experiments in this section, the MEG\n1830\tencoder architecture was kept fixed to the same main configuration with 25 branches and 2 convolutional blocks and\n1831\tonly the feature dimension of the audio target was changed (Fig. 10).\n1832\tWe compare two types of feature reduction. In the PCA condition, we fit a fixed PCA projection on the training audio\n1833\tembeddings, treating all time points from all training segments as samples in the original wav2vec feature space. The\n1834\tMEG model is then trained to predict these PCA-reduced audio targets. This is an unsupervised reduction: it preserves\n1835\thigh-variance directions in the audio embedding distribution, but it does not know which directions are recoverable\n1836\tfrom MEG or useful for retrieval. In the LinearDR condition, the audio embedding is passed through a trainable linear\n1837\tprojection along the feature axis, implemented as a 1 × 1 convolution over feature channels. This projection is optimized\n1838\tjointly with the MEG decoder through the contrastive retrieval loss, so it is allowed to select a task-specific feature\n1839\tsubspace.\n1840\tThe key result is that feature compression is highly effective when the projection is learned with the retrieval objective.\n1841\tA broad range of learned feature dimensions, from 8 to 256, stays close to the unreduced model. The most striking\n1842\tpoint is not the exact optimum, but the flatness of the curve: reducing the target from 768 wav2vec features to only\n1843\t19\n1844\t\n1845\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n1846\t\n1847\tTop-1 accuracy\n1848\t\n1849\t0.6\n1850\t0.5\n1851\t0.4\n1852\t0.3\n1853\t0.2\n1854\t0.1\n1855\t0.9\n1856\t\n1857\tTop-10 accuracy\n1858\t\n1859\t0.8\n1860\t0.7\n1861\t0.6\n1862\tTemporal blocks\n1863\t\n1864\t0.5\n1865\t\n1866\t0 Blocks\n1867\t2 Blocks\n1868\t\n1869\t0.4\n1870\t\n1871\t5 Blocks\n1872\t\n1873\t1.5 s\n1874\t\n1875\t2.25 s\n1876\t\n1877\t3s\n1878\t\n1879\t4s\n1880\t\n1881\t5s\n1882\t\n1883\tSegment duration (s)\n1884\t\n1885\tFigure 8: Test retrieval accuracy as a function of paired MEG–audio segment duration for models with K = 25\n1886\tbranches and B ∈ {0, 2, 5} convolutional blocks. Each point reports final-test accuracy from the checkpoint with the\n1887\tlowest validation loss. Audio embeddings were regenerated directly from the continuous sounds at every duration, and\n1888\tall conditions used the same 5 s-feasible anchor set and the same 991-candidate retrieval database. The 3 s points belong\n1889\tto this regenerated ablation and are distinct from the main 1005-candidate evaluation.\n1890\t\n1891\t12 learned dimensions leaves retrieval accuracy essentially unchanged. This suggests that the model does not need to\n1892\treconstruct the full wav2vec embedding geometry. For the purpose of MEG-to-audio retrieval, most useful alignment is\n1893\tconcentrated in a compact, task-driven linear feature subspace.\n1894\tPower-driven PCA shows a different pattern. It remains competitive only when the retained dimensionality is large, but\n1895\tdegrades much faster in the intermediate and low-dimensional regimes. This separation between PCA and LinearDR is\n1896\tinformative. It shows that the relevant feature directions are not simply the leading variance directions of wav2vec.\n1897\tHigh-variance audio-embedding components are not necessarily the components that are decodable from MEG, and\n1898\tconversely, MEG-relevant components may occupy directions that PCA does not preserve early. Thus, the featurecompression experiment supports a supervised subspace interpretation: the useful target space is low-dimensional, but\n1899\tit must be found through the neural decoding task rather than through variance preservation alone.\n1900\t4.8\n1901\t\n1902\tTemporal-resolution compression\n1903\t\n1904\tWe then performed an analogous experiment along the temporal axis of the target embedding. Here the question is\n1905\tdifferent: can the model retrieve the correct 3-second audio segment from a coarse summary of its wav2vec trajectory,\n1906\tor does it need a temporally resolved target representation?\n1907\tWe evaluated several temporal reductions. Time PCA fits a fixed PCA projection over the sequence length of the\n1908\ttraining audio embeddings, while Time LinearDR uses separate trainable linear maps along the temporal axis for the\n1909\tMEG-derived and audio embeddings. Adaptive average and max pooling reduce the sequence to a fixed number of\n1910\tlocal bins. Mean pooling, max pooling, additive attention pooling, and gated attention pooling each produce one global\n1911\t20\n1912\t\n1913\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n1914\t\n1915\tTop-n retrieval accuracy\n1916\t\n1917\t1.0\n1918\t\n1919\t0.8\n1920\t\n1921\t0.6\n1922\tSegment duration\n1923\t\n1924\t0.4\n1925\t\n1926\t1.5 s\n1927\t2.25 s\n1928\t3s\n1929\t\n1930\t0.2\n1931\t\n1932\t4s\n1933\t5s\n1934\t\n1935\t0.0\n1936\t\n1937\t1\n1938\t\n1939\t5\n1940\t\n1941\t10\n1942\t\n1943\t15\n1944\t\n1945\t20\n1946\t\n1947\t25\n1948\t\n1949\t30\n1950\t\n1951\t35\n1952\t\n1953\t40\n1954\t\n1955\t45\n1956\t\n1957\t50\n1958\t\n1959\tTop-n cutoff\n1960\t\n1961\tFigure 9: Top-n test retrieval accuracy for the main two-block, K = 25 model across paired MEG–audio segment\n1962\tdurations. Curves were calculated using the similarity rank of the correct audio segment among the same 991 candidates.\n1963\tThe advantage of longer segments is present throughout the evaluated range n = 1, . . . , 50.\n1964\t\n1965\tLinearDR\n1966\t\n1967\t40.0\n1968\t\n1969\t70.0\n1970\t\n1971\t37.5\n1972\t\n1973\t67.5\n1974\t\n1975\t35.0\n1976\t\n1977\t65.0\n1978\t\n1979\tTop-10 (%)\n1980\t\n1981\tTop-1 (%)\n1982\t\n1983\tPCA\n1984\t\n1985\t32.5\n1986\t30.0\n1987\t27.5\n1988\t\n1989\t62.5\n1990\t60.0\n1991\t57.5\n1992\t\n1993\t25.0\n1994\t\n1995\t55.0\n1996\t\n1997\t22.5\n1998\t\n1999\t52.5\n2000\t1\n2001\t\n2002\t2\n2003\t\n2004\t4\n2005\t\n2006\t8\n2007\t\n2008\t12 16 32 64 128 256 768\n2009\t\n2010\tFull model\n2011\t\n2012\t1\n2013\t\n2014\tEmbedding dimension\n2015\t\n2016\t2\n2017\t\n2018\t4\n2019\t\n2020\t8\n2021\t\n2022\t12 16 32 64 128 256 768\n2023\t\n2024\tEmbedding dimension\n2025\t\n2026\tFigure 10: Feature-space compression of the target wav2vec representation. The feature dimension is reduced using\n2027\teither a fixed PCA projection or a trainable linear projection optimized with the retrieval loss. Learned feature reduction\n2028\tpreserves retrieval accuracy over a wide range of dimensions, whereas PCA degrades substantially faster in the lowdimensional regime.\n2029\t\n2030\tvector. Query-attention pooling instead uses a learned set of queries and produces a sequence of Q outputs, with\n2031\tQ ∈ {8, 32, 64} in the evaluated conditions (Fig. 11).\n2032\tThe temporal results differ sharply from the feature-compression results. Moderate temporal reduction is possible,\n2033\tespecially with 64 temporal components, but there is no broad low-dimensional plateau comparable to the feature axis.\n2034\tPerformance declines substantially as the temporal representation is shortened, and reducing the sequence to 32 or\n2035\t8 time points causes a clear loss. This indicates that temporal structure is not merely redundant sampling of a static\n2036\tsegment-level representation.\n2037\tThe clearest result is the failure of the single-vector reductions. Mean, max, additive-attention, and gated-attention\n2038\tpooling all produced near-chance retrieval. Query-attention pooling also performed poorly despite retaining a learned\n2039\tsequence of 8, 32, or 64 query outputs. Thus, the result is not limited to the crudeness of simple averaging: both global\n2040\t21\n2041\t\n2042\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n2043\t\n2044\tTop-1 (%)\n2045\t\n2046\t40\n2047\t30\n2048\t20\n2049\t10\n2050\t\n2051\tPCA\n2052\tAdaptive Avg\n2053\tLinearDR\n2054\tAdaptive Max\n2055\tAttention\n2056\tQuery Attention\n2057\tGated Attention\n2058\tMax Pool\n2059\tMean Pool\n2060\t\n2061\t0\n2062\t\n2063\tTop-10 (%)\n2064\t\n2065\t60\n2066\t40\n2067\t20\n2068\t\n2069\tMa\n2070\tx\n2071\tMe\n2072\tan\n2073\t\n2074\t2\n2075\t\n2076\t8\n2077\tMa\n2078\tx8\n2079\tAtt\n2080\tn1\n2081\t28\n2082\tQA\n2083\tttn\n2084\t64\n2085\tQA\n2086\tttn\n2087\t32\n2088\tQA\n2089\tttn\n2090\tGa 8\n2091\tted\n2092\t12\n2093\t8\n2094\t\n2095\tAv\n2096\tg\n2097\t\n2098\tPC\n2099\tA3\n2100\t\n2101\t32\n2102\t\n2103\t2\n2104\t\n2105\tAv\n2106\tg\n2107\t\n2108\tea\n2109\tr3\n2110\t\n2111\t4\n2112\tLin\n2113\t\n2114\tea\n2115\tr6\n2116\t\n2117\t64\n2118\t\n2119\tLin\n2120\t\n2121\tAv\n2122\tg\n2123\t\n2124\tPC\n2125\tA6\n2126\t\n2127\t4\n2128\t\n2129\t0\n2130\t\n2131\tFigure 11: Temporal-resolution compression of the target wav2vec trajectory. Temporal PCA, trainable linear reduction,\n2132\tlocal pooling, and global pooling methods are compared. Unlike feature compression, temporal compression causes a\n2133\tclear performance loss, and global pooling collapses to near-chance retrieval.\n2134\t\n2135\tpooling and severe learned temporal compression discard information needed to identify the correct audio segment.\n2136\tThe model benefits from preserving the within-window trajectory of the speech representation.\n2137\tTogether, the feature and temporal compression experiments reveal an asymmetry in the target embedding. The feature\n2138\taxis contains a compact MEG-decodable subspace that can be learned very efficiently. The temporal axis, in contrast,\n2139\tcarries segment-identifying information that must be preserved at relatively fine resolution. In practical terms, the\n2140\tnetwork can discard many wav2vec feature directions, but it cannot replace the temporal speech trajectory with a global\n2141\tsegment summary.\n2142\t4.9\n2143\t\n2144\tArchitectural ablations\n2145\t\n2146\tWe ablated the main components of our network’s front end to understand how its spatial and temporal factorization\n2147\tcontributes to retrieval. The full model combines a geometry-aware 3D spatial attention layer, a shared unmixing stage,\n2148\tsubject-conditioned spatial mappings, and temporal filtering in the compact branch space. The ablation results are\n2149\tsummarized as changes relative to the full model in Fig. 12.\n2150\tThe strongest qualitative separation is between models that can adapt the spatial projection to individual subjects and\n2151\tmodels that cannot. Variants without the subject-conditioned spatial mapping show a large drop across the board. This\n2152\tis expected in multi-subject MEG: the same cortical activity does not induce identical sensor-space topographies across\n2153\tparticipants, because anatomy, head position, and sensor-source geometry vary. A purely shared sensor-space projection\n2154\ttherefore has to solve two problems at once: align subjects and extract task-relevant components. The ablation shows\n2155\tthat this is too restrictive for the present dataset.\n2156\tWithin the subject-conditioned setting, the remaining ablations show how the rest of the front end improves this aligned\n2157\trepresentation. Spatial attention is important: removing it causes a clear loss, indicating that the model benefits from an\n2158\texplicit shared spatial projection before the subject-conditioned mapping. Replacing 3D attention with 2D attention is\n2159\tless damaging than removing attention altogether, but the 3D version remains the best variant. This supports the use of\n2160\ta sensor-geometry-aware parameterization rather than a flatter spatial representation: the benefit is not only that the\n2161\tmodel has an attention layer, but that this layer is constrained by the physical layout of the MEG sensor array.\n2162\tTemporal filtering provides a further gain when the spatial front end is kept fixed. Replacing the 15-sample temporal\n2163\tfilters with learnable one-sample filters does not collapse retrieval, but it reduces performance. This indicates that\n2164\tthe compact branches benefit from local temporal transformations before nonlinear processing, beyond a learnable\n2165\tinstantaneous channel-wise scaling. In this sense, the 15-sample filters contribute to the intended spatial–temporal\n2166\t22\n2167\t\n2168\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n2169\t\n2170\t2D attention + Unmix + Subject layer + Temporal filter\n2171\t3D attention + Unmix + Subject layer + No temporal filter\n2172\t\n2173\tTop-1\n2174\tTop-10\n2175\t\n2176\t3D attention + Subject layer + Temporal filter\n2177\tNo attention + Unmix + Subject layer + Temporal filter\n2178\tNo attention + Subject layer + Temporal filter\n2179\t3D attention + Unmix + Temporal filter\n2180\tNo attention + Unmix + Temporal filter\n2181\t2D attention + Temporal filter\n2182\t3D attention + Temporal filter\n2183\t25\n2184\t\n2185\t20\n2186\t\n2187\t15\n2188\t\n2189\t10\n2190\t\n2191\tChange vs full model (percentage points)\n2192\t\n2193\t5\n2194\t\n2195\t0\n2196\t\n2197\tFigure 12: Architectural ablations of the network’s front end. Bars show the change in Top-1 and Top-10 retrieval\n2198\taccuracy relative to the full model. The full spatial–temporal factorization performs best, with the largest degradation\n2199\tobserved when subject-conditioned spatial mappings are removed.\n2200\t\n2201\tdecomposition of the model: each branch can select not only where to look in sensor space, but also which local\n2202\ttemporal dynamics are useful for retrieval.\n2203\tThe unmixing layer has a similar role in the spatial factorization. Removing it leads to a measurable drop, suggesting\n2204\tthat the intermediate shared branch space is useful before subject-conditioned adaptation and temporal filtering. The\n2205\teffect is smaller than the loss from removing subject-conditioned mappings, but it is consistent with the design of the\n2206\tarchitecture: the front end works best when shared spatial projection, branch mixing, subject adaptation, and temporal\n2207\tfiltering are all present.\n2208\tOverall, the ablations support the full network’s front-end design. Subject-conditioned spatial mappings provide\n2209\tthe final projection into the K interpretable branches while accounting for cross-subject variability. The 3D spatial\n2210\tattention layer supplies a geometry-aware shared sensor projection before this subject-conditioned mapping, and the\n2211\tunmixing layer refines this shared representation by mixing the attention channels before they are projected into the\n2212\tbranch space. Temporal filters then add branch-wise local temporal selectivity, turning the learned spatial branches into\n2213\tspatial–temporal components.\n2214\t4.10\n2215\t\n2216\tEffect of temporal-filter support\n2217\t\n2218\tThe one-sample ablation establishes that temporal context contributes to retrieval, but does not show whether performance depends on a particular temporal scale. We therefore varied the branch-wise filter support from one to 49\n2219\tsamples (10–490 ms at 100 Hz), while keeping the two-block, K = 25 architecture and training seed fixed (Figure 13).\n2220\tRetrieval depended mainly on whether the filter had sufficient temporal support, rather than on a sharply tuned kernel\n2221\tlength. Relative to the 150 ms default (40.01% Top-1 and 70.60% Top-10), the one-sample and 50 ms variants lost\n2222\tapproximately one percentage point on both metrics, whereas the 90 ms condition reduced the difference to 0.39 and\n2223\t0.72 percentage points, respectively. Across supports from 150 to 490 ms, Top-1 varied by only 0.58 percentage points\n2224\tand Top-10 by 1.17 percentage points. The 490 ms model was numerically best (40.59% Top-1 and 71.77% Top-10),\n2225\tbut the dependence was not monotonic, with the 290 ms condition returning close to the default. Thus, temporal filtering\n2226\tprovides a measurable advantage over instantaneous scaling, while its exact support is only weakly constrained once\n2227\tapproximately 150 ms of context is available. The default therefore lies on a broad performance plateau rather than\n2228\trepresenting a narrowly optimized timescale.\n2229\t\n2230\t5\n2231\t\n2232\tDiscussion\n2233\t\n2234\tWe set out to find which cortical sources support the decoding of perceived speech from MEG, and what in the speech\n2235\tstream the decoder actually uses. The method links the front-end weights of a deep network to three standard quantities:\n2236\t23\n2237\t\n2238\t\fChange vs 150-ms default (pp)\n2239\t\n2240\tInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n2241\t\n2242\t1.0\n2243\t\n2244\tTop-1\n2245\t\n2246\tTop-10\n2247\t\n2248\t0.5\n2249\t0.0\n2250\t0.5\n2251\t1.0\n2252\t1 tap\n2253\t\n2254\t50\n2255\t\n2256\t90\n2257\t\n2258\t150\n2259\t\n2260\t190\n2261\t\n2262\t250\n2263\t\n2264\t290\n2265\t\n2266\tTemporal-filter support (ms; 100 Hz)\n2267\t\n2268\t490\n2269\t\n2270\tFigure 13: Effect of temporal-filter support on retrieval accuracy. Points show changes in Top-1 and Top-10 accuracy\n2271\trelative to the 150 ms default. The one-sample condition contains no temporal context and acts only as branch-wise\n2272\tscaling. All models used the same training seed and validation-based checkpoint selection.\n2273\t\n2274\tsource topographies, activation time series, and power spectra. We treat the front end as a set of branches. Each\n2275\tbranch is a matched spatial-temporal filter, tuned to a neuronal population with its own cortical location and its own\n2276\tsecond-order dynamics. The outputs of these branches feed a stack of convolutional layers that turn them into a wav2vec\n2277\tembedding for retrieval. Having located the sources, we then intervened on the MEG input with paired feature-present\n2278\tand feature-absent substitutions to determine which stimulus-associated information supports retrieval.\n2279\tPreprocessing. We removed ocular and cardiac components from the recordings before training. This was not routine\n2280\thygiene. A network trained to minimize a retrieval loss is potentially opportunistic. It has no reason to prefer a cortical\n2281\tsignal over a peripheral one that predicts the target equally well, and it will take whichever is easier to extract; this\n2282\tis shortcut learning in its plainest form [65]. Eye activity is known to track linguistic structure and attended speech\n2283\teven without corresponding visual input [31–33], while cardiac dynamics vary with narrative intensity and conscious\n2284\tnarrative processing [34, 35]. Left in the data, either could have supplied stimulus-locked information about speech\n2285\tboundaries, and the network would have had every reason to use it. The feature-specific occlusion effects reported\n2286\tabove could then have reflected peripheral rather than cortical information.\n2287\tA compact localizable front end. Our model combines competitive retrieval accuracy with a compact and interpretable brain decoder. Across six seeds, the main configuration (K=25, two convolutional blocks) reaches\n2288\t39.75 ± 0.34% Top-1 and 70.40 ± 0.31% Top-10 among 1005 candidates, while using approximately twenty times\n2289\tfewer trainable parameters than the brain decoder specified by Défossez et al. [1] (Appendix C). Défossez et al. reported\n2290\t41.3% Top-1 and 70.7% Top-10 among 1363 candidates, while Zhang et al. [10] reported Top-1 accuracy between\n2291\t39.6% and 41.9% and Top-10 accuracy between 69.2% and 71.1% among 1464 candidates. The published scores place\n2292\tour model in the same broad performance range but do not support a direct ranking of the architectures, because both the\n2293\tevaluation protocol and preprocessing differ. In both prior studies, each test window was positioned relative to a word\n2294\tonset, placing the onset at a fixed location within the window, whereas our test windows followed a fixed stride and\n2295\twere not aligned to linguistic events. This alignment may provide a consistent temporal cue absent from our evaluation,\n2296\talthough the larger candidate pools used in those studies make retrieval more difficult. We also removed ocular and\n2297\tcardiac components, whereas the other pipelines did not report their explicit removal. Because such components may\n2298\tcarry stimulus-correlated information unrelated to the neural activity of interest, this preprocessing difference may also\n2299\taffect retrieval performance. Taken together, our model achieves comparable accuracy under fixed-stride evaluation\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b9c66524-a39d-41df-a83a-b672ae01d3f5","uuid":"1c00de90-c6dc-4f23-bc77-6cfe976050bb","timestamp":"2026-08-04T16:00:51.971Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/2608.01481v1.txt","content":"0.1\n\n0.0\n\n0\n\n25\n\nFrequency (Hz)\n\n50\n\n0.0\n\n50\n\n100\n\n0\n\nTime (ms)\n\n50\n\n100\n\nTime (ms)\n\n0.1\n\n0\n\n25\n\nFrequency (Hz)\n\n50\n\n0.0\n\n0\n\n25\n\nFrequency (Hz)\n\n50\n\nRH\n\nLH\n\nSpectrum\n\nTemporal pattern\n\nTopography\n\nCluster 7\n\nFigure 6: The 12 largest clusters for the main K = 25 model, computed using the Cable Spool Fort recordings from\nthe first session. Each column shows the medoid of one cluster. Rows show, from top to bottom, the sensor-space\nspatial pattern, the temporal pattern computed using the zero-mean temporal kernel, its magnitude spectrum, and the\ncorresponding MNE-Python [47] source-magnitude estimate on the fsaverage surface in left- and right-hemisphere\nlateral views.\n\n17\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\nRank difference (removal control)\n\n125\n100\n75\n50\n25\n0\n25\n\nS\nHig ilen\nh l ce\nou\ndn\ness\nStr\non\ng a Vo\nco wel\nus\ntic s\non\ns\nFri et\nca\ntiv\nes\nSto\np\nSib s\nHig ilan\nh s ts\nurp\nPse risal\nud\nSil owor\nen\nce d\nHig offse\nhe t\nn\nLiq trop\nuid y\ns/g\nlid\nes\nNa\nWo sals\nrd\non\nset\nS\nSil chw\nen\nce a\nSil edge\nen\nce s\non\ns\nRa et\nre\nwo\nr\nWo d\nrd\nlist\n\n50\n\nGroup effect (+)\n\nGroup effect ( )\n\nSubject\n\nSign-flip null\n\npFWER < 0.05\n\nFigure 7: Paired MEG occlusion effects for 19 stimulus features. For each participant, the plotted effect is the retrievalrank difference between feature-absent replacement (“removal”) and matched feature-present replacement (“control”),\nafter averaging donor realisations, eligible queries, and multiple sessions. Positive values indicate that preserving\nfeature-associated MEG information retained a better rank. Grey points show participant effects and diamonds show\ngroup means. Green violins show the feature-wise sign-flip null distributions in rank-difference units; stars mark\none-sided single-step max-T familywise-error-corrected p < 0.05. Every rank was computed against the complete,\nunchanged bank of 1005 candidates. Because the masks differed in duration and in their sets of eligible queries, effect\nmagnitudes should not be read as a calibrated ranking of feature-encoding strength across features.\n\n4.5\n\nPaired occlusion identifies stimulus-associated MEG information used for retrieval\n\nThe paired rank contrast ∆rf = rf →0 − rf →f was positive after familywise-error correction for 15 of the 19 tested\nfeatures (Figure 7). Thus, for these features, real MEG from feature-present intervals preserved the correct candidate rank\nbetter than equally sized replacements from feature-absent intervals. None of the feature-present controls approached\nrandom ranking: their participant-balanced mean ranks ranged from 23.2 to 153.5, compared with an expected rank of\n503 under random ordering. Because the substitution replaces more MEG for long contiguous events such as silence\nthan for brief ones such as word onsets, the ordering of effect magnitudes across features is confounded by the duration\nof the replaced intervals, so the ranking should be read as identifying which features the decoder uses rather than how\nmuch each contributes.\nThe largest observed contrasts were obtained for silence (∆r = 75.62), high loudness (60.77), vowels (38.70), and\nstrong acoustic onset (36.19); all had pFWER < 10−4 , and the effect was positive in all 27 participants. These results\nshow that the decoder uses MEG distinctions associated with broad speech state, sound intensity, rapid acoustic change,\nand phonetic content.\nFurther corrected positive effects were observed for fricatives (∆r = 12.80), stops (9.27), sibilants (7.94), and high\nsurprisal (7.34). Smaller effects remained significant for pseudowords (1.71, pFWER = 0.022), silence offsets (1.60),\nliquids/glides (1.21), nasals (1.18), word onsets (1.02), schwa (0.96), and the combined silence-edge mask (0.91);\nexcept for pseudowords, these effects had pFWER < 10−4 .\nWe found no corrected positive evidence for high predictive entropy (∆r = 1.40, pFWER = 0.069), silence onsets\n(0.05, pFWER = 0.316), rare words (−2.98), or random word lists (−17.77).\n18\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\nThe observed negativity of the random word-list effect is interesting and given our paired occlusion design we suggest\nthe following interpretation. Since the feature-absent donor for word lists corresponds to unmanipulated narrative\nspeech, narrative MEG substituted into a word-list interval supports retrieval better than the MEG recorded during the\nword-list presentation. Cortical activity recorded during randomly ordered words therefore carries less recoverable\ninformation about the audio than the activity recorded during coherent narrative, which converges with reports of\nreduced neural tracking of language features when sentence and narrative structure are removed [62].\nWe would expect the opposite result as the semantic incongruence is known to supply an additional evoked response\navailable to the decoder, as indexed by the N400 [63, 64]. However a closer look allows us to resolve this potential\ncontradiction. Note that the data set contains two different insertions: pseudowords and the words from the unrelated\nwords list. A pseudoword violates an expectation established by the surrounding sentence, whereas a random word\nlist removes the context that would generate such expectations in the first place. The observed contrast between the\nsmall (yet significant) positive pseudoword effect and the negative word-list effect follows that distinction, and may\nsuggest that what the decoder loses during word lists is not a violation response but the predictive structure that supports\ntracking in coherent speech. The feature-use pattern was remarkably stable across model initializations. All 15 features\nwith a corrected positive effect in the seed-42 model remained positive and met the same criterion in all six trained\nmodels, while the random-word-list contrast remained negative in all six (Appendix B).\n4.6\n\nEffect of segment duration on retrieval\n\nRetrieval accuracy increased monotonically with paired MEG–audio segment duration for all three decoder depths\n(Figure 8). For the main two-block model, Top-1 accuracy increased from 14.37% at 1.5 s to 28.03% at 2.25 s, 39.94%\nat 3 s, 52.66% at 4 s, and 62.20% at 5 s. Top-10 accuracy followed the same progression, increasing from 41.00% to\n59.52%, 70.84%, 80.31%, and 86.33%, respectively.\nThe same duration dependence was present with zero and five convolutional blocks. The five-block model closely\nfollowed the two-block model, reaching 62.38% Top-1 and 86.27% Top-10 accuracy at 5 s. Removing the convolutional\nblocks reduced performance at every duration, although the increase with segment length remained monotonic. The\nzero-block model reached 58.24% Top-1 and 83.37% Top-10 accuracy at 5 s. Thus, the benefit of longer segments was\nrobust to decoder depth, while the downstream convolutional module provided an additional but comparatively modest\nimprovement.\nThe complete rank curves showed that the improvement was not restricted to Top-1 or Top-10 accuracy (Figure 9).\nFor the main two-block model, we estimated Top-n accuracy at different cutoffs from Top-1 to Top-50. The ordering\nby segment duration was preserved at every retrieval cutoff. At n = 50, accuracy increased from 65.88% for 1.5 s\nsegments to 80.26%, 87.32%, 92.47%, and 95.37% for 2.25, 3, 4, and 5 s segments, respectively. Longer paired\nsegments therefore improved both exact retrieval and the broader ranking of the correct audio candidate.\nBecause the MEG input and the audio target were lengthened together, this experiment measures the effect of paired\nsegment duration rather than isolating neural context integration alone. The gains may reflect both the additional\nneural information available in a longer MEG segment and the greater amount or distinctiveness of information in the\ncorresponding audio target.\n4.7\n\nFeature-space compression\n\nWe first tested whether the full wav2vec feature dimension is necessary for retrieval, or whether the MEG-decodable\npart of the target representation lies in a much smaller feature subspace. In all experiments in this section, the MEG\nencoder architecture was kept fixed to the same main configuration with 25 branches and 2 convolutional blocks and\nonly the feature dimension of the audio target was changed (Fig. 10).\nWe compare two types of feature reduction. In the PCA condition, we fit a fixed PCA projection on the training audio\nembeddings, treating all time points from all training segments as samples in the original wav2vec feature space. The\nMEG model is then trained to predict these PCA-reduced audio targets. This is an unsupervised reduction: it preserves\nhigh-variance directions in the audio embedding distribution, but it does not know which directions are recoverable\nfrom MEG or useful for retrieval. In the LinearDR condition, the audio embedding is passed through a trainable linear\nprojection along the feature axis, implemented as a 1 × 1 convolution over feature channels. This projection is optimized\njointly with the MEG decoder through the contrastive retrieval loss, so it is allowed to select a task-specific feature\nsubspace.\nThe key result is that feature compression is highly effective when the projection is learned with the retrieval objective.\nA broad range of learned feature dimensions, from 8 to 256, stays close to the unreduced model. The most striking\npoint is not the exact optimum, but the flatness of the curve: reducing the target from 768 wav2vec features to only\n19\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\nTop-1 accuracy\n\n0.6\n0.5\n0.4\n0.3\n0.2\n0.1\n0.9\n\nTop-10 accuracy\n\n0.8\n0.7\n0.6\nTemporal blocks\n\n0.5\n\n0 Blocks\n2 Blocks\n\n0.4\n\n5 Blocks\n\n1.5 s\n\n2.25 s\n\n3s\n\n4s\n\n5s\n\nSegment duration (s)\n\nFigure 8: Test retrieval accuracy as a function of paired MEG–audio segment duration for models with K = 25\nbranches and B ∈ {0, 2, 5} convolutional blocks. Each point reports final-test accuracy from the checkpoint with the\nlowest validation loss. Audio embeddings were regenerated directly from the continuous sounds at every duration, and\nall conditions used the same 5 s-feasible anchor set and the same 991-candidate retrieval database. The 3 s points belong\nto this regenerated ablation and are distinct from the main 1005-candidate evaluation.\n\n12 learned dimensions leaves retrieval accuracy essentially unchanged. This suggests that the model does not need to\nreconstruct the full wav2vec embedding geometry. For the purpose of MEG-to-audio retrieval, most useful alignment is\nconcentrated in a compact, task-driven linear feature subspace.\nPower-driven PCA shows a different pattern. It remains competitive only when the retained dimensionality is large, but\ndegrades much faster in the intermediate and low-dimensional regimes. This separation between PCA and LinearDR is\ninformative. It shows that the relevant feature directions are not simply the leading variance directions of wav2vec.\nHigh-variance audio-embedding components are not necessarily the components that are decodable from MEG, and\nconversely, MEG-relevant components may occupy directions that PCA does not preserve early. Thus, the featurecompression experiment supports a supervised subspace interpretation: the useful target space is low-dimensional, but\nit must be found through the neural decoding task rather than through variance preservation alone.\n4.8\n\nTemporal-resolution compression\n\nWe then performed an analogous experiment along the temporal axis of the target embedding. Here the question is\ndifferent: can the model retrieve the correct 3-second audio segment from a coarse summary of its wav2vec trajectory,\nor does it need a temporally resolved target representation?\nWe evaluated several temporal reductions. Time PCA fits a fixed PCA projection over the sequence length of the\ntraining audio embeddings, while Time LinearDR uses separate trainable linear maps along the temporal axis for the\nMEG-derived and audio embeddings. Adaptive average and max pooling reduce the sequence to a fixed number of\nlocal bins. Mean pooling, max pooling, additive attention pooling, and gated attention pooling each produce one global\n20\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\nTop-n retrieval accuracy\n\n1.0\n\n0.8\n\n0.6\nSegment duration\n\n0.4\n\n1.5 s\n2.25 s\n3s\n\n0.2\n\n4s\n5s\n\n0.0\n\n1\n\n5\n\n10\n\n15\n\n20\n\n25\n\n30\n\n35\n\n40\n\n45\n\n50\n\nTop-n cutoff\n\nFigure 9: Top-n test retrieval accuracy for the main two-block, K = 25 model across paired MEG–audio segment\ndurations. Curves were calculated using the similarity rank of the correct audio segment among the same 991 candidates.\nThe advantage of longer segments is present throughout the evaluated range n = 1, . . . , 50.\n\nLinearDR\n\n40.0\n\n70.0\n\n37.5\n\n67.5\n\n35.0\n\n65.0\n\nTop-10 (%)\n\nTop-1 (%)\n\nPCA\n\n32.5\n30.0\n27.5\n\n62.5\n60.0\n57.5\n\n25.0\n\n55.0\n\n22.5\n\n52.5\n1\n\n2\n\n4\n\n8\n\n12 16 32 64 128 256 768\n\nFull model\n\n1\n\nEmbedding dimension\n\n2\n\n4\n\n8\n\n12 16 32 64 128 256 768\n\nEmbedding dimension\n\nFigure 10: Feature-space compression of the target wav2vec representation. The feature dimension is reduced using\neither a fixed PCA projection or a trainable linear projection optimized with the retrieval loss. Learned feature reduction\npreserves retrieval accuracy over a wide range of dimensions, whereas PCA degrades substantially faster in the lowdimensional regime.\n\nvector. Query-attention pooling instead uses a learned set of queries and produces a sequence of Q outputs, with\nQ ∈ {8, 32, 64} in the evaluated conditions (Fig. 11).\nThe temporal results differ sharply from the feature-compression results. Moderate temporal reduction is possible,\nespecially with 64 temporal components, but there is no broad low-dimensional plateau comparable to the feature axis.\nPerformance declines substantially as the temporal representation is shortened, and reducing the sequence to 32 or\n8 time points causes a clear loss. This indicates that temporal structure is not merely redundant sampling of a static\nsegment-level representation.\nThe clearest result is the failure of the single-vector reductions. Mean, max, additive-attention, and gated-attention\npooling all produced near-chance retrieval. Query-attention pooling also performed poorly despite retaining a learned\nsequence of 8, 32, or 64 query outputs. Thus, the result is not limited to the crudeness of simple averaging: both global\n21\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\nTop-1 (%)\n\n40\n30\n20\n10\n\nPCA\nAdaptive Avg\nLinearDR\nAdaptive Max\nAttention\nQuery Attention\nGated Attention\nMax Pool\nMean Pool\n\n0\n\nTop-10 (%)\n\n60\n40\n20\n\nMa\nx\nMe\nan\n\n2\n\n8\nMa\nx8\nAtt\nn1\n28\nQA\nttn\n64\nQA\nttn\n32\nQA\nttn\nGa 8\nted\n12\n8\n\nAv\ng\n\nPC\nA3\n\n32\n\n2\n\nAv\ng\n\nea\nr3\n\n4\nLin\n\nea\nr6\n\n64\n\nLin\n\nAv\ng\n\nPC\nA6\n\n4\n\n0\n\nFigure 11: Temporal-resolution compression of the target wav2vec trajectory. Temporal PCA, trainable linear reduction,\nlocal pooling, and global pooling methods are compared. Unlike feature compression, temporal compression causes a\nclear performance loss, and global pooling collapses to near-chance retrieval.\n\npooling and severe learned temporal compression discard information needed to identify the correct audio segment.\nThe model benefits from preserving the within-window trajectory of the speech representation.\nTogether, the feature and temporal compression experiments reveal an asymmetry in the target embedding. The feature\naxis contains a compact MEG-decodable subspace that can be learned very efficiently. The temporal axis, in contrast,\ncarries segment-identifying information that must be preserved at relatively fine resolution. In practical terms, the\nnetwork can discard many wav2vec feature directions, but it cannot replace the temporal speech trajectory with a global\nsegment summary.\n4.9\n\nArchitectural ablations\n\nWe ablated the main components of our network’s front end to understand how its spatial and temporal factorization\ncontributes to retrieval. The full model combines a geometry-aware 3D spatial attention layer, a shared unmixing stage,\nsubject-conditioned spatial mappings, and temporal filtering in the compact branch space. The ablation results are\nsummarized as changes relative to the full model in Fig. 12.\nThe strongest qualitative separation is between models that can adapt the spatial projection to individual subjects and\nmodels that cannot. Variants without the subject-conditioned spatial mapping show a large drop across the board. This\nis expected in multi-subject MEG: the same cortical activity does not induce identical sensor-space topographies across\nparticipants, because anatomy, head position, and sensor-source geometry vary. A purely shared sensor-space projection\ntherefore has to solve two problems at once: align subjects and extract task-relevant components. The ablation shows\nthat this is too restrictive for the present dataset.\nWithin the subject-conditioned setting, the remaining ablations show how the rest of the front end improves this aligned\nrepresentation. Spatial attention is important: removing it causes a clear loss, indicating that the model benefits from an\nexplicit shared spatial projection before the subject-conditioned mapping. Replacing 3D attention with 2D attention is\nless damaging than removing attention altogether, but the 3D version remains the best variant. This supports the use of\na sensor-geometry-aware parameterization rather than a flatter spatial representation: the benefit is not only that the\nmodel has an attention layer, but that this layer is constrained by the physical layout of the MEG sensor array.\nTemporal filtering provides a further gain when the spatial front end is kept fixed. Replacing the 15-sample temporal\nfilters with learnable one-sample filters does not collapse retrieval, but it reduces performance. This indicates that\nthe compact branches benefit from local temporal transformations before nonlinear processing, beyond a learnable\ninstantaneous channel-wise scaling. In this sense, the 15-sample filters contribute to the intended spatial–temporal\n22\n\n\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\n2D attention + Unmix + Subject layer + Temporal filter\n3D attention + Unmix + Subject layer + No temporal filter\n\nTop-1\nTop-10\n\n3D attention + Subject layer + Temporal filter\nNo attention + Unmix + Subject layer + Temporal filter\nNo attention + Subject layer + Temporal filter\n3D attention + Unmix + Temporal filter\nNo attention + Unmix + Temporal filter\n2D attention + Temporal filter\n3D attention + Temporal filter\n25\n\n20\n\n15\n\n10\n\nChange vs full model (percentage points)\n\n5\n\n0\n\nFigure 12: Architectural ablations of the network’s front end. Bars show the change in Top-1 and Top-10 retrieval\naccuracy relative to the full model. The full spatial–temporal factorization performs best, with the largest degradation\nobserved when subject-conditioned spatial mappings are removed.\n\ndecomposition of the model: each branch can select not only where to look in sensor space, but also which local\ntemporal dynamics are useful for retrieval.\nThe unmixing layer has a similar role in the spatial factorization. Removing it leads to a measurable drop, suggesting\nthat the intermediate shared branch space is useful before subject-conditioned adaptation and temporal filtering. The\neffect is smaller than the loss from removing subject-conditioned mappings, but it is consistent with the design of the\narchitecture: the front end works best when shared spatial projection, branch mixing, subject adaptation, and temporal\nfiltering are all present.\nOverall, the ablations support the full network’s front-end design. Subject-conditioned spatial mappings provide\nthe final projection into the K interpretable branches while accounting for cross-subject variability. The 3D spatial\nattention layer supplies a geometry-aware shared sensor projection before this subject-conditioned mapping, and the\nunmixing layer refines this shared representation by mixing the attention channels before they are projected into the\nbranch space. Temporal filters then add branch-wise local temporal selectivity, turning the learned spatial branches into\nspatial–temporal components.\n4.10\n\nEffect of temporal-filter support\n\nThe one-sample ablation establishes that temporal context contributes to retrieval, but does not show whether performance depends on a particular temporal scale. We therefore varied the branch-wise filter support from one to 49\nsamples (10–490 ms at 100 Hz), while keeping the two-block, K = 25 architecture and training seed fixed (Figure 13).\nRetrieval depended mainly on whether the filter had sufficient temporal support, rather than on a sharply tuned kernel\nlength. Relative to the 150 ms default (40.01% Top-1 and 70.60% Top-10), the one-sample and 50 ms variants lost\napproximately one percentage point on both metrics, whereas the 90 ms condition reduced the difference to 0.39 and\n0.72 percentage points, respectively. Across supports from 150 to 490 ms, Top-1 varied by only 0.58 percentage points\nand Top-10 by 1.17 percentage points. The 490 ms model was numerically best (40.59% Top-1 and 71.77% Top-10),\nbut the dependence was not monotonic, with the 290 ms condition returning close to the default. Thus, temporal filtering\nprovides a measurable advantage over instantaneous scaling, while its exact support is only weakly constrained once\napproximately 150 ms of context is available. The default therefore lies on a broad performance plateau rather than\nrepresenting a narrowly optimized timescale.\n\n5\n\nDiscussion\n\nWe set out to find which cortical sources support the decoding of perceived speech from MEG, and what in the speech\nstream the decoder actually uses. The method links the front-end weights of a deep network to three standard quantities:\n23\n\n\fChange vs 150-ms default (pp)\n\nInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n\n1.0\n\nTop-1\n\nTop-10\n\n0.5\n0.0\n0.5\n1.0\n1 tap\n\n50\n\n90\n\n150\n\n190\n\n250\n\n290\n\nTemporal-filter support (ms; 100 Hz)\n\n490\n\nFigure 13: Effect of temporal-filter support on retrieval accuracy. Points show changes in Top-1 and Top-10 accuracy\nrelative to the 150 ms default. The one-sample condition contains no temporal context and acts only as branch-wise\nscaling. All models used the same training seed and validation-based checkpoint selection.\n\nsource topographies, activation time series, and power spectra. We treat the front end as a set of branches. Each\nbranch is a matched spatial-temporal filter, tuned to a neuronal population with its own cortical location and its own\nsecond-order dynamics. The outputs of these branches feed a stack of convolutional layers that turn them into a wav2vec\nembedding for retrieval. Having located the sources, we then intervened on the MEG input with paired feature-present\nand feature-absent substitutions to determine which stimulus-associated information supports retrieval.\nPreprocessing. We removed ocular and cardiac components from the recordings before training. This was not routine\nhygiene. A network trained to minimize a retrieval loss is potentially opportunistic. It has no reason to prefer a cortical\nsignal over a peripheral one that predicts the target equally well, and it will take whichever is easier to extract; this\nis shortcut learning in its plainest form [65]. Eye activity is known to track linguistic structure and attended speech\neven without corresponding visual input [31–33], while cardiac dynamics vary with narrative intensity and conscious\nnarrative processing [34, 35]. Left in the data, either could have supplied stimulus-locked information about speech\nboundaries, and the network would have had every reason to use it. The feature-specific occlusion effects reported\nabove could then have reflected peripheral rather than cortical information.\nA compact localizable front end. Our model combines competitive retrieval accuracy with a compact and interpretable brain decoder. Across six seeds, the main configuration (K=25, two convolutional blocks) reaches\n39.75 ± 0.34% Top-1 and 70.40 ± 0.31% Top-10 among 1005 candidates, while using approximately twenty times\nfewer trainable parameters than the brain decoder specified by Défossez et al. [1] (Appendix C). Défossez et al. reported\n41.3% Top-1 and 70.7% Top-10 among 1363 candidates, while Zhang et al. [10] reported Top-1 accuracy between\n39.6% and 41.9% and Top-10 accuracy between 69.2% and 71.1% among 1464 candidates. The published scores place\nour model in the same broad performance range but do not support a direct ranking of the architectures, because both the\nevaluation protocol and preprocessing differ. In both prior studies, each test window was positioned relative to a word\nonset, placing the onset at a fixed location within the window, whereas our test windows followed a fixed stride and\nwere not aligned to linguistic events. This alignment may provide a consistent temporal cue absent from our evaluation,\nalthough the larger candidate pools used in those studies make retrieval more difficult. We also removed ocular and\ncardiac components, whereas the other pipelines did not report their explicit removal. Because such components may\ncarry stimulus-correlated information unrelated to the neural activity of interest, this preprocessing difference may also\naffect retrieval performance. Taken together, our model achieves comparable accuracy under fixed-stride evaluation","numLines":700,"startLine":1600,"totalLines":3131}}}
