{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b91620abf00c4a67","type":"tool_result","content":"2300\tand explicit artifact removal, while using a compact decoder that exposes directly interpretable spatial and temporal\n2301\tcomponents.\n2302\tOur own ablation grid supports a controlled comparison because every configuration in it shares the same data,\n2303\tpreprocessing, and test set. The K = 270, five-block configuration is the closest tested model in branch count and\n2304\t24\n2305\t\n2306\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n2307\t\n2308\tdecoder depth. Despite containing 14.8 times more trainable parameters, it scores 3.60 percentage points lower in Top-1\n2309\tand 3.14 points lower in Top-10 than the main model (Appendix C). The remaining architectural differences were\n2310\texamined separately. Replacing the 3D spherical-harmonic attention with the 2D version costs about one percentage\n2311\tpoint as well as removing the branch-wise temporal filters, see Figure 12. Interestingly, the filter-length sweep shows\n2312\tthe second effect from the other side, see Figure 13: the one-tap condition, which carries no temporal context, sits\n2313\tabout one point below the 150 ms default, while extending the support to 490 ms yields the highest performance, adding\n2314\t0.58 percentage points in Top-1 and 1.17 percentage points in Top-10. Retrieval improves overall as temporal support\n2315\tincreases up to approximately 150 ms, whereas gains from longer filters are less systematic. Filter length therefore\n2316\tmatters, but its benefit begins to saturate around this scale. At K = 25, increasing decoder depth from two to five\n2317\tblocks reduces Top-1 by 0.95 and Top-10 by 0.78 percentage points. At five blocks, increasing K from 25 to 270 costs\n2318\ta further 2.65 and 2.36 percentage points, respectively; see Figure 3. Each effect is larger than the 0.34 percentage\n2319\tpoint spread we measure across training seeds. We did not train the exact conjunction of 2D attention, no temporal\n2320\tfiltering, K = 270, and five convolutional blocks, so interactions between these choices cannot be excluded, but their\n2321\tseparate comparisons all favor the main design. That margin is not the main point. The front end is built on the physics\n2322\tof the measurement and the physiology of the sources, and it is that grounding, not the extra few points, which makes\n2323\tthe interpretation below possible.\n2324\tThe capacity result can also be considered from a point of view more conventional in MEG neuroimaging. Accuracy\n2325\trises from a few branches to a plateau at K ≈ 10–25, and adding channels toward K=270 hurts rather than helps. From\n2326\ta linear-algebra perspective, task-related M/EEG activity is known to occupy a low-dimensional subspace of the sensor\n2327\tspace. Dipole modeling uses this through the signal subspace [45], which is defined by SVD and is therefore driven by\n2328\tpower. Spatio-spectral decomposition [39], source-power comodulation methods [40], and compact factorized networks\n2329\tsuch as EEGNet, ShallowConvNet and LF-CNN [28–30] instead find a subspace driven by information. Those networks\n2330\twere built for simpler classification and regression tasks. Our results show that the same limit holds when the target is\n2331\tcontrastive alignment to a high-dimensional speech embedding.\n2332\tCortical mapping. Although the public MEG-MASC release includes participant-specific T1-weighted scans for\n2333\ta subset of participants, defaced with PyDeface [7], most of the scans available to us were truncated by defacing,\n2334\tand FreeSurfer surface reconstruction succeeded for only six participants, too few to support the cross-participant\n2335\taggregation reported here. We therefore mapped every participant through one template brain and a shared coregistration.\n2336\tRAP-MUSIC dipole fitting [45] applied to the spatial patterns derived from the front-end weights returns sources in\n2337\tbilateral auditory cortex and the frontal lobe, see Figure 5b.\n2338\tThe distributed source maps in Figure 6 further detail the picture. The dominant foci lie along the superior and middle\n2339\ttemporal gyri, and several clusters carry additional foci anterior and superior to these, within peri-Sylvian cortex.\n2340\tIndividual clusters are strongly lateralized, with both left-dominant and right-dominant components. This distribution is\n2341\tconsistent with the cortical network described for auditory speech perception, with accounts of auditory–conceptual\n2342\tintegration [57] and predictive coding in speech [56], and with the dual-stream organization of speech processing [55].\n2343\tWe refrain from assigning individual clusters to named cortical areas. The maps are common-template estimates,\n2344\tand the separation between the anterior peri-Sylvian foci and their neighbours is smaller than the spatial uncertainty\n2345\tintroduced by the shared coregistration. A parcellation-based assignment on individual anatomy is the natural next step.\n2346\tCiferri et al. [66] found a similar distribution when mapping Whisper layers onto ECoG. The two pipelines differ in\n2347\tmodality and in speech model, yet they cover overlapping territory.\n2348\tIn the temporal patterns and spectra in Figure 6 low-frequency (most likely evoked) activity dominates. This agrees\n2349\twith temporal response studies of speech tracking, and with work on the role of slow oscillations in parsing the speech\n2350\tenvelope and phrase structure [49, 50, 56, 60, 67]. Some cluster medoids also show alpha and lower-beta contributions,\n2351\tin line with earlier reports on these rhythms in natural speech perception [61].\n2352\tWhat makes a speech segment identifiable? The occlusion results show that the decoder identifies speech segments\n2353\tfrom MEG information spanning several levels of the stimulus rather than from a single privileged linguistic representation. Retrieval depended on broad acoustic state, rapid changes in the sound, phonetic content, and selected contextual\n2354\tproperties of words. Because the paired comparison held the replaced intervals and substitution procedure constant\n2355\twithin each feature, these effects cannot be explained solely by generic damage caused by replacing part of the MEG\n2356\tsignal. They nevertheless reflect information associated with each tested feature, rather than an independent causal\n2357\tcontribution of that feature, because several stimulus properties overlap and covary.\n2358\tThe prominent effects for silence, high loudness, and strong acoustic onsets indicate that the decoder relies both on\n2359\tsustained acoustic state and on changes that segment the continuous speech stream. Speech pauses supply stable\n2360\tlandmarks within a continuous narrative, while loudness and spectral flux distinguish the acoustic content surrounding\n2361\tthem. Cortical tracking of speech edges and the envelope is well established in invasive and non-invasive recordings\n2362\t25\n2363\t\n2364\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n2365\t\n2366\t[49–54, 68], and inserting pauses between words strengthens the relationship between the speech envelope and neural\n2367\tactivity [69]. Our intervention results show that the decoder uses MEG information associated with both the speech–\n2368\tsilence state and changes within the audible signal. They should not be read as showing that larger sound amplitude\n2369\titself increases a neural response or model sensitivity.\n2370\tAt the phonetic level, vowels produced the largest contrast, and positive corrected effects were also found for stops,\n2371\tfricatives, sibilants, nasals, liquids/glides, and schwa. For these analyses, the feature-absent donor state consisted\n2372\tof other labelled phonemes, so the contrast cannot be explained merely by replacing speech with silence. It instead\n2373\tindicates that MEG distinctions associated with phoneme classes help the model identify the matching audio segment.\n2374\tThe effects nevertheless do not form a calibrated phonetic hierarchy: the classes overlap (for example, sibilants are\n2375\ta subset of fricatives and schwa is included among vowels), and their masks occupy different fractions of the test\n2376\twindows.\n2377\tThe linguistic results were more selective. High-surprisal words produced a clear positive effect, consistent with\n2378\tevidence that contextual prediction statistics are represented during continuous speech [62, 70–73]. Word onsets and\n2379\tpseudoword insertions also produced smaller corrected effects. By contrast, predictive entropy, lexical rarity, and\n2380\trandom word-list intervals provided no corrected positive evidence in the pre-specified direction. The analysis therefore\n2381\tsupports a contribution of contextual unexpectedness, but not the broader claim that every lexical or narrative disruption\n2382\timproves retrieval. The word-list contrast was negative in all six independently trained models (Appendix B), and we\n2383\tread that in Section 4.5 as reduced recoverable information during speech that retains its words but loses its structure.\n2384\tThe consequence for the present argument is narrower than the finding itself: contextual structure enters retrieval by\n2385\tsupporting tracking, not by supplying anomaly responses that the decoder can exploit. An account in which every\n2386\tdeparture from expectation adds usable signal would predict the opposite sign, and does not survive the intervention.\n2387\tThe feature-axis compression experiments complement, but do not calibrate, these occlusion effects. Reducing the\n2388\t768-dimensional wav2vec feature axis to about a dozen dimensions with a learned linear map leaves retrieval accuracy\n2389\tnearly unchanged, whereas power-driven PCA degrades much earlier. Because each reduced dimensionality was trained\n2390\tas a separate model rather than obtained by projecting the reported one, the twelve-dimensional configuration is itself\n2391\ta working decoder: it retains full retrieval accuracy while shrinking the convolutional head from K × 768 × 3 to\n2392\tK × 12 × 3 parameters and reducing the complete learned system to 436,879 trainable parameters (Appendix C). The\n2393\tdominant reductions relative to the original architecture nevertheless come from the branch count and the convolutional\n2394\tdepth rather than from the target dimension. This indicates that MEG is aligned to a compact task-selected subspace\n2395\trather than to the full wav2vec geometry or its highest-variance directions, consistent with reports that brain responses\n2396\talign to particular layers or subspaces of self-supervised speech models [66, 74, 75]. The temporal axis behaves\n2397\tdifferently: global pooling of the within-segment trajectory reduces retrieval to near chance. Together, the occlusion\n2398\tand compression analyses indicate that the model combines temporally structured acoustic, phonetic, and contextual\n2399\tinformation within a compact learned target subspace.\n2400\tInterpretable architectural choices. The front-end ablations, see Figure 12, connect these findings to design choices\n2401\tthat Défossez et al. [1] introduced for engineering reasons and that we kept in an interpretable form. Removing the\n2402\tsubject-conditioned spatial mappings produces the largest drop. Anatomy, head position and sensor geometry differ\n2403\tacross people, and multi-subject MEG decoding has to absorb that difference; a single matrix multiplication per subject\n2404\tturns out to be enough, consistent with [76]. Removing the attention layer altogether costs four to five percentage points.\n2405\tReplacing the 3D spherical-harmonic attention with the 2D version reduces the score by around one point, which is,\n2406\thowever, still above the 0.34 percentage point spread we measure across seeds. At the same time, the stronger argument\n2407\tfor the spherical parameterization is physical rather than empirical: MEG sensors lie on a roughly spherical surface,\n2408\tand spherical harmonics are the natural basis for a field sampled that way [6]. Retrieval improves overall as temporal\n2409\tsupport increases up to approximately 150 ms. Beyond this scale, longer filters do not produce stable further gains,\n2410\talthough the best individual result is obtained at 490 ms. Thus, filter length matters, but its benefit appears to begin\n2411\tsaturating once the model captures temporal structure over approximately 150 ms. Their main value here, though, is\n2412\tinterpretive. These filters are what give access to the second-order dynamics of each population. Without them a branch\n2413\tis characterized by its topography alone and no conclusions about the type of activity can be made.\n2414\tWhat retrieval accuracy actually measures. Taken together, the results argue for treating retrieval accuracy as a\n2415\tcomposite score rather than as a direct assay of linguistic content. A model can reach the operating range of Défossez\n2416\tet al. by combining a compact peri-Sylvian spatial subspace with MEG distinctions associated with silence, sound\n2417\tintensity, acoustic onsets, phoneme classes, and contextual surprisal, all aligned to a thin task-selected subspace\n2418\tof a pretrained speech embedding whose temporal trajectory must remain intact. This combination fits decades of\n2419\tspeech-envelope tracking and predictive-processing work, but it is not the same as reading out phonemes one by one, or\n2420\tmeaning alone, from single trials of MEG. The random-word-list result shows that these contributions are not simply\n2421\t26\n2422\t\n2423\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n2424\t\n2425\tadditive. In the paired occlusion analysis (Section 4.5; Figure 7), replacing MEG recorded during random word lists\n2426\twith MEG from coherent narrative improved retrieval (∆r = −17.77), even though the word-list intervals retained\n2427\tindividual spoken words and their local acoustic and phonetic cues. This contrast suggests that sentence and narrative\n2428\tcontext support the neural tracking on which retrieval relies, rather than contributing merely another independently\n2429\tdecodable feature. Retrieval performance on connected speech therefore should not be expected to transfer unchanged\n2430\tto word-list material.\n2431\tThe strong acoustic-state effects and the failure of heavy temporal compression suggest one reason audio-aligned\n2432\tobjectives may be advantageous relative to text-aligned objectives on non-invasive data: the audio target preserves\n2433\tpause structure and fine temporal variation that MEG carries, whereas token-level representations remove much of that\n2434\tdirect alignment. Importantly, the interpretable architecture does not lower the headline score here; it changes what that\n2435\tscore can be taken to mean.\n2436\tThis matters because the two available alternatives leave a gap. Most high-performance deep networks optimize\n2437\taccuracy and leave the decision rule opaque; their weights do not translate into cortical sources and the dynamics of\n2438\ttheir activity. Classical neuroimaging does identify the neural substrate of behavior-specific states, but usually in a\n2439\tnon-ecological setting and with models too simple to capture the subtle variations that identify segments in the natural\n2440\tcontinuous speech. An interpretable front end closes that gap from both sides. It keeps a compact decoder that matches\n2441\tcurrent retrieval benchmarks, while returning branch-wise topographies and spectra that can be read as physiologically\n2442\tmeaningful structure.\n2443\tApplied to a corpus with individual anatomy, the same pipeline becomes a practical tool for time-resolved imaging and\n2444\tknowledge discovery from multichannel neurophysiological data collected during complex, ecologically valid tasks.\n2445\t\n2446\t6\n2447\t\n2448\tLimitations\n2449\t\n2450\tOur analyses used a single MEG corpus [7] with 27 participants. The participant sign-flip test therefore supports\n2451\tgeneralization across listeners conditional on the fixed held-out audio material; it does not establish that the same\n2452\tfeature-use pattern will replicate for new narratives. Repeating the paired occlusion analysis across six independently\n2453\ttrained instances of the same architecture showed that the reported feature-use pattern was not specific to the seed-42\n2454\tsolution (Appendix B). This check addresses dependence on model initialization, but not robustness to a different\n2455\tarchitecture, corpus, or evaluation protocol. Cross-seed agreement is informative in its own right: a feature effect\n2456\treproduced across independently trained solutions indicates stable reliance on the corresponding feature-associated\n2457\tMEG distinction, whereas variation across seeds indicates less stable reliance across trained solutions. Because each\n2458\tfeature is evaluated using the same mask duration in every seed, this analysis does not disentangle the stability of feature\n2459\tuse from the mask-duration confound.\n2460\tInterpretation of the front end assumes linear space–time factorized processing in the first layer. This may miss more\n2461\tcomplex source dynamics, such as space–time inseparable cortical waves. Although a plurality of branches can represent\n2462\tsome propagating activity, the present interpretation cannot establish the role of cortical waves per se.\n2463\tThe paired substitutions use real MEG from the same participant and session, and the feature-present arm controls for\n2464\tgeneric replacement damage, but the intervention does not isolate a strictly independent causal contribution of each\n2465\tstimulus variable. Several annotations overlap or covary, their masks differ in duration and eligible-window coverage,\n2466\tand donor replacement may alter correlated stimulus states. The reported rank contrasts should therefore be interpreted\n2467\tas evidence that the trained decoder uses MEG information distinguishing the feature-present and feature-absent states,\n2468\trather than as directly comparable estimates of neural encoding strength across features.\n2469\tRAP-MUSIC localizes the full signal subspace at once, and both it and the clustered spatial-temporal profiles were\n2470\tmapped through the single generic forward model discussed above. Anatomical variability across participants will\n2471\ttherefore have shifted the estimated sites and produced more spread-out cortical maps than individual forward models\n2472\twould have given. Individual forward models could nevertheless be built for the six participants whose defaced\n2473\tvolumes survived surface reconstruction. Comparing branch localizations obtained from individual anatomy with the\n2474\tcommon-template estimates reported here would bound the spatial error introduced by the shared coregistration, and\n2475\twe intend to pursue this.\n2476\t\n2477\tAcknowledgements\n2478\tThis work is an output of a research project HSE-BR-2025-26 implemented as part of the Basic Research Program\n2479\tat HSE University. This research was supported in part through computational resources of HPC facilities at HSE\n2480\tUniversity. The authors thank the contributors of the MEG-MASC dataset [7] for making the data publicly available.\n2481\t27\n2482\t\n2483\t\fInterpretable MEG Decoding of Perceived Speech\n2484\t\n2485\tThe authors are grateful to Alexey Voskoboynikov for the original version of the code partly reproducing the results of\n2486\t[1].\n2487\t\n2488\tData and code availability\n2489\tThe MEG-MASC dataset is publicly available at [7]. Our code is available at https://github.com/ivsemenkov/\n2490\tLISA/. ICA components required to clean the MEG-MASC dataset are available at https://osf.io/3wrft/.\n2491\t\n2492\tEthics statement\n2493\tThe study used publicly available MEG data [7] whose collection was approved by the Institutional Review Board (IRB)\n2494\tethics committee of New York University Abu Dhabi. The authors of the current manuscript declare no competing\n2495\tinterests.\n2496\t\n2497\tDeclaration of generative AI and AI-assisted technologies in the manuscript preparation\n2498\tprocess\n2499\tDuring the preparation of this work, the authors used LLMs for stylistic editing and computer code development\n2500\tassistance. The authors reviewed and edited the output as needed and take full responsibility for the content of the\n2501\tpublished article.\n2502\t\n2503\tReferences\n2504\t[1] Alexandre Défossez, Charlotte Caucheteux, Jérémy Rapin, Ori Kabeli, and Jean-Rémi King. Decoding speech\n2505\tperception from non-invasive brain recordings. Nature Machine Intelligence, 5(10):1097–1107, 2023.\n2506\t[2] Alexei Baevski, Yuhao Zhou, Abdelrahman Mohamed, and Michael Auli. wav2vec 2.0: A framework for selfsupervised learning of speech representations. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, volume 33,\n2507\tpages 12449–12460, 2020.\n2508\t[3] Alec Radford, Jong Wook Kim, Chris Hallacy, Aditya Ramesh, Gabriel Goh, Sandhini Agarwal, Girish Sastry,\n2509\tAmanda Askell, Pamela Mishkin, Jack Clark, Gretchen Krueger, and Ilya Sutskever. Learning transferable visual\n2510\tmodels from natural language supervision. In Marina Meila and Tong Zhang, editors, Proceedings of the 38th\n2511\tInternational Conference on Machine Learning, volume 139 of Proceedings of Machine Learning Research, pages\n2512\t8748–8763. PMLR, 18–24 Jul 2021.\n2513\t[4] Artur Petrosyan, Mikhail Sinkin, Mikhail Lebedev, and Alexei Ossadtchi. Decoding and interpreting cortical\n2514\tsignals with a compact convolutional neural network. Journal of Neural Engineering, 18(2):026019, 2021.\n2515\t[5] Artur Petrosyan, Alexey Voskoboinikov, Dmitrii Sukhinin, Anna Makarova, Anastasia Skalnaya